module Examples.Bootstrap

-- Phase 7.9a integration: the first **3-stage bootstrap compiler**
-- written entirely in ll-lang itself. Stitches a real parser, a real
-- elaborator, and a minimal HM-style type checker into ONE program:
-- source Str -> lexer -> module parser -> `List[Decl]` -> elaborator
-- (name resolution + exhaustiveness) -> minimal type-check pass
-- (arithmetic + if) -> list of `E001` / `E002` / `E003` error strings
-- -> printed to stdout. Showcase milestone: the first time THREE
-- compiler layers authored in ll-lang share a single AST and run
-- back-to-back on a real source string.
--
-- Phase 7.9a delta vs 17-pipeline-real.lll:
--   * Add a minimal `TypeExpr` ADT (`TyName Str | TyVar Str`).
--   * Add a simple `typeEq` structural equality over `TypeExpr`.
--   * Add `inferExprType : Expr -> TypeExpr` that assigns the obvious
--     type to `EInt` / `EStr` / arithmetic / `EIf` branches and falls
--     through to `TyVar "?"` everywhere else (no lambdas, no let-gen,
--     no unify, no Subst — that's Phase 7.9b).
--   * Add `typeCheck : Expr -> List[Str]` which walks the expression
--     and emits `E001 TypeMismatch <l> vs <r>` strings for each
--     `+ - * /` or `if` branch whose operand types don't match.
--   * Extend `elaborate` to also run the HM pass over every fn body
--     and concatenate the resulting error list.
--   * Add a new `fn badType(x Int) Int = x + "y"` to the hardcoded
--     driver source so the HM pass has something to fire on. The
--     expected stdout now has four lines:
--       E002 UnboundVar undefinedName
--       E003 NonExhaustiveMatch Shape missing Circle
--       E003 NonExhaustiveMatch Shape missing Rect
--       E001 TypeMismatch Int vs Str
--
-- Deliberately SKIPPED in this slice (punted to Phase 7.9b):
--   * Full HM with unify / Subst / fresh vars.
--   * `ELam` / `ELetIn` / `EMatch` / `ECon` type checking — the HM
--     pass just skips these shapes (treats them as `TyVar "?"`).
--   * Codegen integration — 7.9b will fold in `19-codegen-real.lll`.
--
-- Inherited header from 17-pipeline-real.lll follows (parser + elab
-- halves are unchanged from that file):
--
-- Phase 7.6 integration: the first end-to-end compiler pipeline written
-- entirely in ll-lang itself. Stitches `15-moduleparser-real.lll` and
-- `16-elaborator-real.lll` into a single program: source Str -> lexer
-- -> module parser -> `List[Decl]` -> elaborator (name resolution +
-- exhaustiveness) -> list of `E002` / `E003` error strings -> printed
-- to stdout. Showcase milestone — the first time two compiler layers
-- authored in ll-lang share a single AST and run back-to-back on a real
-- source string.
--
-- The file is big (parser + elaborator in one) but mechanically
-- assembled: the parser half is copied verbatim from
-- `15-moduleparser-real.lll`; the elaborator half is adapted from
-- `16-elaborator-real.lll` to walk 15's richer `Decl` / `Expr` / `Pat`
-- AST instead of 16's minimal local AST.
--
-- Adaptation deltas vs 16:
--   * `checkExpr` handles every 15 `Expr` variant: `EInt` / `EStr` /
--     `EVar` (the only leaf that actually resolves names) / `ETagged`
--     / all four arithmetic arms / `EApp` / `ELam` / `ELetIn` / `EIf`
--     / `EMatch`.
--   * `collectDecl` / `checkDecl` / `exhaustivenessDecl` unwrap 15's
--     `DType TypeDecl` / `DFn FnDecl` / `DLet LetDecl` / `DTag Str` /
--     `DImport Str` / `DExport Decl` variants. `DExport inner`
--     recurses into the inner decl so an exported fn still registers
--     its top-level name and gets its body walked.
--   * `exhaustivenessDecl` reads the last curried param's type name
--     via `MkParam name (TR typeName)` on 15's `Param`. 15's `Pat`
--     set has no `PCon` (named constructor patterns are a later
--     slice), so `coveredCtors` is always empty — a match over a
--     declared sum type with no catch-all arm emits one `E003` per
--     constructor. This is the deliberately narrow cut that keeps the
--     integration tight.
--   * `DImport` contributes nothing to the env (real module resolution
--     is a much later phase); `DLet`'s RHS is walked now (16 skipped
--     it).
--
-- The inherited parser half still carries the Phase 7.1..7.5e doc
-- strings — kept as-is so diffing against `15-moduleparser-real.lll`
-- remains trivial.
--
-- Original 15 header follows:
--
-- Phase 7.4 / 7.5a / 7.5b / 7.5c / 7.5d: a real recursive-descent **full
-- module** parser written in ll-lang itself. Showcase milestone — the
-- first single file that can consume a whole `module M\n type ...\n
-- let ... \n fn ... = ...` source end-to-end and emit a `List[Decl]`
-- AST. Stitches together the earlier self-hosting slices:
--   * 09-lexer-real.lll       — single-pass char tokenizer
--   * 12-typeparser-real.lll  — single-line sum-type decls
--   * 13-fnparser-real.lll    — curried typed fn decls + arithmetic body
--   * 14-exprparser-real.lll  — `if-then-else`, `match`, var refs,
--                               `let-in` chains, lambdas
--
-- Phase 7.5a added `let name = expr` decls at module level and a
-- `match scrut with | pat -> body | ...` special form inside fn bodies
-- (lifting the match machinery from 14-exprparser-real.lll).
--
-- Phase 7.5b added two more expression forms inside fn bodies:
-- `\x. body` lambdas and `let name = e1 in e2` let-in chains. Both
-- dispatch from `parseExpr`, so either can nest inside any other
-- expression form the grammar already accepts. Note: `TKwLet` is now
-- ambiguous at dispatch time — `parseDecls` consumes it as a module-
-- level decl, `parseExpr` (invoked from inside an fn body) consumes it
-- as an expression let-in. Context disambiguates cleanly because the
-- two entry points are independent.
--
-- Phase 7.5c added two more items from the Phase 7.5 backlog: **string
-- literals** in fn-body expressions (`EStr s`, lexed as `TStr` via the
-- `takeStrBody` helper lifted from 14-exprparser-real.lll) and **cons
-- / nil patterns** in match arms (`PNil` for `[]`, `PCons h t` for
-- `h :: t`, right-associative via recursive `parsePat`). The lexer
-- gained `TLBrack` / `TRBrack` / `TColonColon` tokens; `parseAtom` got
-- a `TStr` arm; `parsePat` was split into `parsePrimaryPat` (which
-- handles `[]` as a primary) plus a recursive wrapper that folds a
-- trailing `:: pat` into `PCons`.
--
-- Phase 7.5d added two more items: **tagged literals** in expression
-- position (`ETagged e tag`, surface `"x"[UserId]` / `5[m]`, restricted
-- to `EInt` / `EStr` atoms to mirror the host compiler's Phase 7.2.2
-- literal-only rule) and **parametric ctor args** in type decls
-- (`TAApp head args`, bracket-form surface `Maybe[Int]` / `Result[A][E]`
-- — picked over the `(Maybe Int)` juxtaposition form because brackets
-- need no lookahead). The lexer already has `TLBrack` / `TRBrack` from
-- Phase 7.5c; `parseAtom` got a `maybeTagged` post-pass after `EInt`
-- and `EStr`; `parseTypeArgs` was split into `parseOneTypeArg` +
-- `parseBrackArgs` so bracket groups can chain and nest.
--
-- Phase 7.5e adds three more module-level decl forms — closing out the
-- last item on the Phase 7.5 umbrella: bare **`tag Name`** decls
-- (`DTag`, no body, no params), dotted **`import Foo.Bar.Baz`** decls
-- (`DImport`, path built via the same `parseModuleNameTail` helper the
-- module header uses), and an **`export`** modifier prefix that wraps
-- any existing decl in a new `DExport` variant. The lexer gains three
-- keywords `tag` / `import` / `export`; `parseDecls` grows three new
-- dispatch arms (the `export` arm reads the next decl via a new
-- `parseOneDecl` helper and wraps the result in `DExport`); the
-- pretty-printer gains three trivial arms (`tag <name>`, `import
-- <path>`, `export <show inner>`). `trait` / `impl` decls are
-- deliberately NOT in this slice — they're much more complex
-- (multi-line signatures + indented fn bodies) and live in a separate
-- Phase 7.6 slice.
--
-- Still constrained: no multi-line fn bodies, no `trait`/`impl`
-- module-level decls, no multi-line type decls, no list-literal
-- expression atoms (`[a b c]` in expression position), no tuple
-- patterns.
--
-- What IS covered is enough to prove the front-end can be a single
-- ll-lang program:
--   * module header with dot-separated TypeId segments
--   * multiple single-line sum-type decls with multi-ctor `|` chains
--   * multiple module-level `let name = expr` decls
--   * multiple single-line fn decls with curried typed params, optional
--     return type, int-literal / var-ref / arithmetic / if-then-else /
--     match-with-scrutinee / let-in / lambda / application bodies
--
-- Grammar (each decl on its own line; `\n` between decls):
--   module   = 'module' Upper ('.' Upper)* '\n' decls
--   decls    = decl*
--   decl     = typeDecl | letDecl | fnDecl | tagDecl | importDecl
--            | 'export' decl
--   tagDecl  = 'tag' Upper
--   importDecl = 'import' Upper ('.' Upper)*
--   typeDecl = 'type' Upper param* '=' ctor ('|' ctor)*
--   param    = single-uppercase-letter (A, B, ...)
--   ctor     = Upper typeArg*
--   typeArg  = Upper brackGroup*       -- Phase 7.5d: `Maybe[Int]` chain
--   brackGroup = '[' typeArg ']'       -- recursive, flattened into TAApp
--   letDecl  = 'let' lower '=' expr
--   fnDecl   = 'fn' lower paramGroup* typeName? '=' expr
--   paramGroup = '(' lower typeName ')'  |  '(' ')'
--   expr     = ifExpr | matchExpr | letInExpr | lamExpr | addSub
--   ifExpr   = 'if' expr 'then' expr 'else' expr
--   matchExpr = 'match' expr 'with' ('|' pat '->' addSub)+
--   letInExpr = 'let' lower '=' expr 'in' expr
--   lamExpr  = '\' lower '.' expr
--   pat      = primaryPat ('::' pat)?
--   primaryPat = int | lower | '_' | '[' ']'
--   addSub   = mulDiv (('+' | '-') mulDiv)*
--   mulDiv   = app (('*' | '/') app)*
--   app      = atom atom*
--   atom     = int tag? | str tag? | lower | Upper | '(' expr ')'
--   tag      = '[' Upper ']'           -- Phase 7.5d: ETagged wrapper
--
-- The pretty printer produces a deterministic, unambiguous string so the
-- runtime test can substring-match each line of the expected output.

type Maybe A = Some A | None

type Token =
  | TKwModule
  | TKwType
  | TKwFn
  | TKwIf
  | TKwThen
  | TKwElse
  | TKwLet
  | TKwIn
  | TKwMatch
  | TKwWith
  | TKwTag
  | TKwImport
  | TKwExport
  | TLower Str
  | TUpper Str
  | TInt Int
  | TStr Str
  | TChar Char
  | TLParen
  | TRParen
  | TLBrack
  | TRBrack
  | TEq
  | TEqEq
  | TLt
  | TGt
  | TBar
  | TDot
  | TBackslash
  | TArrow
  | TUnder
  | TColonColon
  | TPlus
  | TMinus
  | TStar
  | TSlash
  | TNewline
  | TEnd

-- Type-decl AST ------------------------------------------------------------

-- Phase 7.5d: `TAApp head args` is the bracket-form parametric type
-- application `Maybe[Int]` / `Result[A][E]`. Multiple bracket groups on
-- the same head flatten into one `TAApp` with a multi-element arg list.
-- Plain single-segment names still parse as `TAVar` (single letter) or
-- `TACon` (multi-char) exactly like earlier slices.
type TypeArg =
  | TAVar Str
  | TACon Str
  | TAApp Str List[TypeArg]

type Ctor = MkCtor Str List[TypeArg]

type TypeDecl = MkTypeDecl Str List[Str] List[Ctor]

-- Fn-decl + expression AST -------------------------------------------------

type TypeRef = TR Str

type Param = MkParam Str TypeRef

-- Match patterns for this slice: int literal, lowercase var binder,
-- wildcard `_`, nil `[]`, cons `h :: t`, and named constructor `PCon
-- name args`. Phase 7.5c added `PNil` / `PCons`; Phase 7.9l added
-- `PCon` for arms like `| Some n -> ...` / `| None -> ...`. String-
-- literal patterns, list literals, and tuple patterns still live in
-- Phase 7.5+'s backlog.
type Pat =
  | PInt Int
  | PStr Str
  | PVar Str
  | PWild
  | PNil
  | PCons Pat Pat
  | PCon Str List[Pat]

-- Expression grammar for this slice: int literal, var ref (covers both
-- lowercase identifiers and uppercase constructor references — the
-- elaborator would distinguish, but for pretty-printing they're the
-- same leaf), parenthesised grouping, `+ - * /`, `if-then-else`, curried
-- juxtaposition application, AND `match scrut with | pat -> body | ...`.
-- Match arms are stored as two parallel lists inside EMatch (pats, bodies)
-- the same way 14-exprparser-real.lll does it — the current codegen
-- can't emit mutually-recursive `type MatchArm = MkArm Pat Expr`, so
-- splitting the arm into two lockstep lists dodges the issue and keeps
-- the invariant `length pats == length bodies` by construction.
type Expr =
  | EInt Int
  | EStr Str
  | EChar Char
  | EVar Str
  | ETagged Expr Str
  | EAdd Expr Expr
  | ESub Expr Expr
  | EMul Expr Expr
  | EDiv Expr Expr
  | EEq Expr Expr
  | ELt Expr Expr
  | EGt Expr Expr
  | EApp Expr Expr
  | ELam Str Expr
  | ELetIn Str Expr Expr
  | EIf Expr Expr Expr
  | EMatch Expr List[Pat] List[Expr]
  | ENil
  | ECons Expr Expr

-- Phase 7.5a: `let name = expr` at module level. Lives alongside type and
-- fn decls inside the top-level `Decl` sum.
type LetDecl = MkLet Str Expr

type FnDecl = MkFn Str List[Param] Maybe[TypeRef] Expr

-- Module AST ---------------------------------------------------------------

-- Phase 7.5e adds three more decl variants:
--   * `DTag name` for bare `tag UserId` decls (semantic tag
--     declaration, no body, no params);
--   * `DImport path` for `import Std.List` decls, where `path` is the
--     dot-joined qualified module name (built by walking the sequence
--     of `TUpper` / `TDot` tokens exactly like `parseModuleHeader`);
--   * `DExport inner` — a wrapper marking the inner decl as publicly
--     visible. The `export` keyword is a modifier prefix on top of any
--     existing decl (`export fn ...`, `export type ...`, `export let
--     ...`), so `DExport` just holds a nested `Decl`. Picked over an
--     `exported: Bool` field on each variant because wrapping doesn't
--     touch any existing variant shape.
type Decl =
  | DType TypeDecl
  | DLet LetDecl
  | DFn FnDecl
  | DTag Str
  | DImport Str
  | DExport Decl

type Module = MkModule Str List[Decl]

-- ---------- Char predicates ---------------------------------------------

-- ASCII uppercase / lowercase tests, same shape as 12/13/14. ll-lang lacks
-- `&&` so each predicate is two nested `if`s.
fn isUpperChar(c Char) Bool =
  let n = charToInt c in
  if n < 65 then false
  else if n > 90 then false
  else true

fn isLowerChar(c Char) Bool =
  let n = charToInt c in
  if n < 97 then false
  else if n > 122 then false
  else true

fn isIdStart(c Char) Bool =
  if isUpperChar c then true else isLowerChar c

fn isIdCont(c Char) Bool =
  if isIdStart c then true else charIsDigit c

-- ---------- Lexer --------------------------------------------------------

-- Greedy take/drop while predicate. Phase 7.9s: inlined into four
-- monomorphic helpers (one per predicate) to sidestep the host HM's
-- refusal to apply a rigid type variable to an argument. Each fn has
-- exactly one typed param, so no untyped-param ambiguity remains.
fn takeIdCont(cs List[Char]) List[Char] =
  match cs with
    | c :: rest -> if isIdCont c then listAppend [c] (takeIdCont rest) else []
    | _ -> []

fn dropIdCont(cs List[Char]) List[Char] =
  match cs with
    | c :: rest -> if isIdCont c then dropIdCont rest else cs
    | _ -> []

fn takeDigit(cs List[Char]) List[Char] =
  match cs with
    | c :: rest -> if charIsDigit c then listAppend [c] (takeDigit rest) else []
    | _ -> []

fn dropDigit(cs List[Char]) List[Char] =
  match cs with
    | c :: rest -> if charIsDigit c then dropDigit rest else cs
    | _ -> []

-- Unwrap `strToInt` into a raw Int; the lexer only calls this on strings
-- that are already all-digits, so the `None` branch is defensive.
fn parseIntStr(s Str) Int =
  match strToInt s with
    | Some n -> n
    | None -> 0

-- Classify an identifier: keywords map to TKw*; uppercase-start becomes
-- TUpper (type / ctor names); lowercase-start becomes TLower (fn names,
-- params, value vars). Phase 7.5a adds `let` (module-level decls) and
-- `match` / `with` (match-as-expression inside fn bodies). Phase 7.5b
-- adds `in` (let-in chains inside fn bodies, dispatched from parseExpr).
-- Phase 7.5e adds `tag` / `import` / `export` — three new module-level
-- decl forms (a bare `tag Name` decl, a dotted `import Foo.Bar` decl,
-- and an `export` modifier prefix that wraps any existing decl in a
-- `DExport` variant).
fn classifyIdent(s Str) Token =
  match s with
    | "module" -> TKwModule
    | "type" -> TKwType
    | "fn" -> TKwFn
    | "if" -> TKwIf
    | "then" -> TKwThen
    | "else" -> TKwElse
    | "let" -> TKwLet
    | "in" -> TKwIn
    | "match" -> TKwMatch
    | "with" -> TKwWith
    | "tag" -> TKwTag
    | "import" -> TKwImport
    | "export" -> TKwExport
    | _ ->
      let cs = strChars s in
      match cs with
        | c :: _ -> if isUpperChar c then TUpper s else TLower s
        | _ -> TLower s

fn lexId(cs List[Char]) List[Token] =
  let idChars = takeIdCont cs in
  let leftover = dropIdCont cs in
  let tok = classifyIdent (strFromChars idChars) in
  listAppend [tok] (lexChars leftover)

fn lexNum(cs List[Char]) List[Token] =
  let digits = takeDigit cs in
  let leftover = dropDigit cs in
  listAppend [TInt (parseIntStr (strFromChars digits))] (lexChars leftover)

-- Collect a string literal body: the opening `"` has already been
-- consumed, now greedily take chars until the closing `"`. Returns
-- (body-chars, leftover). Lifted from 14-exprparser-real.lll.
--
-- Phase 7.9m: detect `\` inside the body and delegate to
-- `takeStrBodyEsc`, which consumes+decodes the next char via
-- `decodeEscape` (reused from 7.9j). This lets `"say \"hi\"\n"`
-- round-trip without the `\"` terminating the string prematurely.
fn takeStrBody(cs List[Char]) =
  | c :: rest ->
    if c == '"' then ([], rest)
    else if c == '\\' then takeStrBodyEsc rest
    else
      let (body, leftover) = takeStrBody rest in
      (c :: body, leftover)
  | _ -> ([], [])

-- Phase 7.9m: consume one escape inside a string body. The `\` has
-- already been consumed by `takeStrBody`; `cs` points at the escape
-- content char. Decode it via `decodeEscape`, then recurse into
-- `takeStrBody` to keep collecting the rest of the body.
fn takeStrBodyEsc(cs List[Char]) =
  | esc :: rest ->
    let (body, leftover) = takeStrBody rest in
    (decodeEscape esc :: body, leftover)
  | _ -> ([], [])

fn lexStr(cs List[Char]) List[Token] =
  let (body, leftover) = takeStrBody cs in
  listAppend [TStr (strFromChars body)] (lexChars leftover)

-- Phase 7.9j: decode one escape-sequence content char. Supports the
-- escapes needed for self-host: `\n` → newline, `\t` → tab,
-- `\\` → backslash, `\'` → single-quote. Phase 7.9m extends this
-- with `\"` → double-quote for string-literal escape support.
-- Unknown escapes pass through raw (lenient — mirrors the catch-all
-- in `lexChars`).
fn decodeEscape(c Char) Char =
  if c == 'n' then '\n'
  else if c == 't' then '\t'
  else if c == '\\' then '\\'
  else if c == '\'' then '\''
  else if c == '"' then '"'
  else c

-- Phase 7.9j: lex the tail of an escaped single-char literal
-- `'\e'`. The opening `'` AND the backslash have already been
-- consumed; `cs` points at the escape content char. Consume the
-- content char, decode it, expect the closing `'`, and emit a
-- `TChar (decodeEscape esc)` token. Missing closing quote → drop.
fn lexCharEsc(cs List[Char]) List[Token] =
  match cs with
    | esc :: rest ->
      match rest with
        | c :: r ->
          if c == '\'' then listAppend [TChar (decodeEscape esc)] (lexChars r)
          else lexChars cs
        | _ -> lexChars cs
    | _ -> []

-- Phase 7.9i: lex a single-char literal `'c'`. The opening `'` has
-- already been consumed. Consume one content char, expect the
-- closing `'`, and emit a `TChar ch` token. Phase 7.9j extends this
-- to detect a leading `\` and dispatch to `lexCharEsc` for the four
-- supported escapes (`\n` / `\t` / `\\` / `\'`). If the closing `'`
-- is missing (unterminated), silently fall through and re-lex from
-- the saved position, matching the leniency of the catch-all
-- `else lexChars rest` arm in `lexChars`.
fn lexCharLit(cs List[Char]) List[Token] =
  match cs with
    | ch :: rest ->
      if ch == '\\' then lexCharEsc rest
      else
        match rest with
          | c :: r ->
            if c == '\'' then listAppend [TChar ch] (lexChars r)
            else lexChars cs
          | _ -> lexChars cs
    | _ -> []

-- Handle `:` vs `::`: peek the next char to decide. Only `::` is a
-- valid token in this grammar (cons pattern separator); a bare `:` is
-- not used anywhere, so it falls through silently.
fn lexColonOrCons(cs List[Char]) List[Token] =
  | c :: rest ->
    if c == ':' then listAppend [TColonColon] (lexChars rest)
    else lexChars cs
  | _ -> [TEnd]

-- Handle `-` vs `->` vs `--`: peek the next char to decide between
-- TMinus, TArrow, and a line comment. `->` is used by match arms
-- (`| pat -> body`); 15 only had TMinus before Phase 7.5a. Phase 7.9n
-- adds the `--` line-comment arm: if the char after `-` is another
-- `-`, hand off to `skipLineComment` which consumes everything up to
-- (and including) the next `\n`, then resumes `lexChars` on what
-- follows. Without this, a single commented line desyncs `parseDecls`
-- and silently drops every subsequent decl — fatal for fixpoint
-- since the bootstrap's own source begins with ~190 lines of
-- `-- header comment` before the first real decl.
fn skipLineComment(cs List[Char]) List[Token] =
  | c :: rest ->
    if c == '\n' then listAppend [TNewline] (lexChars rest)
    else skipLineComment rest
  | _ -> [TEnd]

fn lexMinusOrArrow(cs List[Char]) List[Token] =
  | c :: rest ->
    if c == '>' then listAppend [TArrow] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '-' then skipLineComment rest
    else listAppend [TMinus] (lexChars cs)
  | _ -> [TMinus]

-- Phase 7.9f: handle `=` vs `==`. Main loop already consumed the first
-- `=` before calling this helper. If the next char is also `=`, emit a
-- single `TEqEq` and recurse past both. Otherwise emit `TEq` and
-- re-lex starting at the current head (don't drop the non-`=` char).
fn lexEqOrEqEq(cs List[Char]) List[Token] =
  | c :: rest ->
    if c == '=' then listAppend [TEqEq] (lexChars rest)
    else listAppend [TEq] (lexChars cs)
  | _ -> [TEq]

-- Main loop. Newlines are significant (they separate decls); spaces and
-- tabs are skipped. Every other single-char symbol maps directly to its
-- token constructor. Phase 7.5a adds `_` → TUnder (wildcard pattern) and
-- the two-char `->` arrow via the `lexMinusOrArrow` helper. Phase 7.5b
-- adds `\` → TBackslash (lambda-intro) — `.` → TDot was already here.
fn lexChars(cs List[Char]) List[Token] =
  | c :: rest ->
    if c == '\n' then listAppend [TNewline] (lexChars rest)
    else if charIsSpace c then lexChars rest
    else if isIdStart c then lexId cs
    else if charIsDigit c then lexNum cs
    else if c == '"' then lexStr rest
    else if c == '\'' then lexCharLit rest
    else if c == '(' then listAppend [TLParen] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == ')' then listAppend [TRParen] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '[' then listAppend [TLBrack] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == ']' then listAppend [TRBrack] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '=' then lexEqOrEqEq rest
    else if c == '<' then listAppend [TLt] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '>' then listAppend [TGt] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '|' then listAppend [TBar] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '.' then listAppend [TDot] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '\\' then listAppend [TBackslash] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '_' then listAppend [TUnder] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == ':' then lexColonOrCons rest
    else if c == '+' then listAppend [TPlus] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '-' then lexMinusOrArrow rest
    else if c == '*' then listAppend [TStar] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '/' then listAppend [TSlash] (lexChars rest)
    else lexChars rest
  | _ -> [TEnd]

fn tokenize(src Str) List[Token] = lexChars (strChars src)

-- ---------- Recursive-descent parser ------------------------------------

-- Skip leading newlines between decls. Blank lines are invisible.
fn skipNewlines(toks List[Token]) List[Token] =
  | TNewline :: rest -> skipNewlines rest
  | _ -> toks

-- Module name tail: `.Upper` segments. Each recursive call prepends the
-- dot + next segment to the accumulator string, then keeps walking. On
-- the first non-dot token it stops.
fn parseModuleNameTail(acc Str)(toks List[Token]) =
  | TDot :: TUpper seg :: rest ->
    let acc2 = strConcat (strConcat acc ".") seg in
    parseModuleNameTail acc2 rest
  | _ -> (acc, toks)

-- `module Upper (. Upper)*`. Returns (qualified-name, remaining-tokens).
fn parseModuleHeader(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwModule :: TUpper head :: rest ->
    parseModuleNameTail head rest
  | _ -> ("?", toks)

-- Top-level driver. Reads the module header, then drives a decl loop.
fn parseModule(toks List[Token]) Module =
  let (name, rest) = parseModuleHeader toks in
  let decls = parseDecls (skipNewlines rest) in
  MkModule name decls

-- Main decl loop. Cons-pattern fast paths dispatch on the first token.
-- Anything that isn't `type`, `let`, `fn`, `tag`, `import`, or `export`
-- terminates the loop — TEnd, TNewline (already skipped), unexpected
-- tokens all fall through to the wildcard. Phase 7.5a added the `let`
-- arm; Phase 7.5e adds three more: `tag Name`, `import Foo.Bar`, and
-- the `export` modifier prefix. The `export` arm parses the remainder
-- of the token stream as any regular decl via `parseOneDecl` and wraps
-- the result in `DExport` — this keeps the modifier composable with
-- every existing decl form without duplicating the dispatch ladder.
fn parseDecls(toks List[Token]) List[Decl] =
  | TKwType :: _ -> consTypeDecl (parseTypeDecl toks)
  | TKwLet :: _ -> consLetDecl (parseLetDecl toks)
  | TKwFn :: _ -> consFnDecl (parseFnDecl toks)
  | TKwTag :: _ -> consDecl (parseTagDecl toks)
  | TKwImport :: _ -> consDecl (parseImportDecl toks)
  | TKwExport :: rest ->
    let (inner, rest2) = parseOneDecl rest in
    DExport inner :: parseDecls (skipNewlines rest2)
  | _ -> []

-- Helper: turn a (TypeDecl, leftover) pair into the recursive list.
fn consTypeDecl(pair) List[Decl] =
  let (td, rest) = pair in
  DType td :: parseDecls (skipNewlines rest)

-- Same shape for let decls (Phase 7.5a).
fn consLetDecl(pair) List[Decl] =
  let (ld, rest) = pair in
  DLet ld :: parseDecls (skipNewlines rest)

fn consFnDecl(pair) List[Decl] =
  let (fd, rest) = pair in
  DFn fd :: parseDecls (skipNewlines rest)

-- Phase 7.5e: generic `(Decl, leftover) -> List[Decl]` cons helper for
-- parsers that already return a fully-constructed `Decl` (tag and
-- import decls build `DTag` / `DImport` directly, unlike the type /
-- let / fn paths whose helpers return the inner decl body).
fn consDecl(pair) List[Decl] =
  let (d, rest) = pair in
  d :: parseDecls (skipNewlines rest)

-- Phase 7.5e: parse exactly one decl, returning `(Decl, leftover)`.
-- Used by the `TKwExport :: rest` arm in `parseDecls` to consume the
-- decl that follows an `export` keyword without recursing into the
-- full decl-list loop. Dispatches on the same keyword ladder as
-- `parseDecls` and returns the single parsed decl + whatever tokens
-- are left. `export export ...` falls through to a defensive `DTag
-- "?"` arm — the grammar forbids stacking, and an error there would
-- immediately surface at the next parseDecls call.
fn parseOneDecl(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwType :: _ ->
    let (td, rest) = parseTypeDecl toks in
    (DType td, rest)
  | TKwLet :: _ ->
    let (ld, rest) = parseLetDecl toks in
    (DLet ld, rest)
  | TKwFn :: _ ->
    let (fd, rest) = parseFnDecl toks in
    (DFn fd, rest)
  | TKwTag :: _ -> parseTagDecl toks
  | TKwImport :: _ -> parseImportDecl toks
  | _ -> (DTag "?", toks)

-- ---------- Tag-decl parser (Phase 7.5e) --------------------------------

-- Single `tag Name` decl. No body, no params — just the keyword and a
-- single TUpper. Mirrors the host compiler's KwTag arm in parseDecl.
fn parseTagDecl(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwTag :: TUpper name :: rest -> (DTag name, rest)
  | _ -> (DTag "?", toks)

-- ---------- Import-decl parser (Phase 7.5e) -----------------------------

-- Single `import Foo.Bar.Baz` decl. The dotted tail is collected by
-- `parseModuleNameTail` — the same helper `parseModuleHeader` uses, so
-- the qualified path string is built exactly the same way on both
-- sides. Stores the path as one dot-joined string, matching how the
-- pretty printer later renders it.
fn parseImportDecl(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwImport :: TUpper head :: rest ->
    let (path, rest2) = parseModuleNameTail head rest in
    (DImport path, rest2)
  | _ -> (DImport "?", toks)

-- ---------- Let-decl parser (Phase 7.5a) --------------------------------

-- Single module-level let decl: 'let' lower '=' expr. The RHS reuses the
-- full `parseExpr` pipeline so it can be any expression the fn-body
-- grammar accepts (int lit, arithmetic, if-then-else, match, ...).
fn parseLetDecl(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwLet :: TLower name :: TEq :: rest ->
    -- Phase 7.9.newlines: tolerate `let x =\n  expr` module-level decls.
    let rest0 = skipNewlines rest in
    let (body, rest2) = parseExpr rest0 in
    (MkLet name body, rest2)
  | _ -> (MkLet "?" (EInt 0), toks)

-- ---------- Type-decl parser (adapted from 12-typeparser-real.lll) ------

-- Single decl: 'type' Upper param* '=' ctor ('|' ctor)*
fn parseTypeDecl(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwType :: TUpper name :: rest ->
    let (prms, rest2) = parseTypeParams rest in
    let rest3 = match rest2 with
      | TEq :: r -> skipNewlines r
      | _ -> rest2
    in
    let (ctors, rest4) = parseCtors rest3 in
    (MkTypeDecl name prms ctors, rest4)
  | _ -> (MkTypeDecl "?" [] [], toks)

-- Collect zero or more single-letter uppercase type params, stopping at
-- TEq (or anything else non-Upper).
fn parseTypeParams(toks List[Token]) =
  | TUpper s :: rest ->
    if strLen s == 1 then
      let (ps, rest2) = parseTypeParams rest in
      (s :: ps, rest2)
    else ([], toks)
  | _ -> ([], toks)

-- First ctor is mandatory; each subsequent ctor is preceded by `|`.
-- Phase 7.9o: tolerate multi-line form with newlines before/between
-- ctors and an optional leading `|` before the first ctor.
fn parseCtors(toks List[Token]) =
  let toks2 = skipNewlines toks in
  let toks3 = match toks2 with
    | TBar :: r -> skipNewlines r
    | _ -> toks2
  in
  let (c, rest) = parseCtor toks3 in
  parseCtorsTail [c] rest

fn parseCtorsTail(acc List[Ctor])(toks List[Token]) =
  match skipNewlines toks with
    | TBar :: rest ->
      let rest2 = skipNewlines rest in
      let (c, rest3) = parseCtor rest2 in
      parseCtorsTail (listAppend acc [c]) rest3
    | _ -> (acc, toks)

fn parseCtor(toks List[Token]) =
  | TUpper name :: rest ->
    let (args, rest2) = parseTypeArgs rest in
    (MkCtor name args, rest2)
  | _ -> (MkCtor "?" [], toks)

-- Collect zero or more type args. Stops on TBar / TNewline / TEnd / any
-- non-Upper token. Each arg is parsed via `parseOneTypeArg`, which
-- handles both the bare form (`Int`, `A`) and the Phase 7.5d bracket-
-- form parametric application (`Maybe[Int]`, `Result[A][E]`).
fn parseTypeArgs(toks List[Token]) =
  | TUpper _ :: _ ->
    let (arg, rest) = parseOneTypeArg toks in
    let (args, rest2) = parseTypeArgs rest in
    (arg :: args, rest2)
  | _ -> ([], toks)

-- Phase 7.5d: parse a single type arg. Bare TUpper becomes `TAVar` (len
-- 1) or `TACon` (longer). If the head is immediately followed by one or
-- more bracket groups `[ typeArg ]`, those groups flatten into a single
-- `TAApp head args` ctor. Recurses through `parseBrackArgs` so a
-- bracket group's inner arg can itself be another bracket-form
-- application (`Foo[Bar[Baz]]`).
fn parseOneTypeArg(toks List[Token]) =
  | TUpper s :: rest ->
    let (brackArgs, rest2) = parseBrackArgs [] rest in
    if listIsEmpty brackArgs then
      let arg = if strLen s == 1 then TAVar s else TACon s in
      (arg, rest2)
    else (TAApp s brackArgs, rest2)
  | _ -> (TACon "?", toks)

-- Collect successive `[ typeArg ]` bracket groups, appending each
-- parsed inner arg to the running `acc` list. Stops on the first
-- non-TLBrack token. The caller is responsible for deciding whether the
-- resulting arg list should become a `TAApp` or a plain bare form.
fn parseBrackArgs(acc List[TypeArg])(toks List[Token]) =
  | TLBrack :: rest ->
    let (inner, rest2) = parseOneTypeArg rest in
    let rest3 = match rest2 with
      | TRBrack :: r -> r
      | _ -> rest2
    in
    parseBrackArgs (listAppend acc [inner]) rest3
  | _ -> (acc, toks)

-- ---------- Fn-decl parser (adapted from 13-fnparser-real.lll) ----------

-- Single fn decl: 'fn' lower paramGroup* typeName? '=' expr
fn parseFnDecl(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwFn :: TLower name :: rest ->
    let (prms, rest2) = parseParamGroups rest in
    let (retTy, rest3) = parseReturnType rest2 in
    let rest4 = match rest3 with
      | TEq :: r -> skipNewlines r
      | _ -> rest3
    in
    let (body, rest5) = parseExpr rest4 in
    (MkFn name prms retTy body, rest5)
  | _ -> (MkFn "?" [] None (EInt 0), toks)

-- Phase 7.9d: after a head `TUpper` has been read in param / return
-- type position, consume any trailing `[ ... ]` bracket groups (e.g.
-- the `[Int]` in `Maybe[Int]`, or the `[A][E]` in `Result[A][E]`) and
-- return just the leftover token list. We don't carry the bracket
-- payload through the AST in this slice — `TypeRef = TR Str` only
-- holds the head name today, and neither the elaborator nor the F#
-- codegen inspect type args. The sole goal is to keep `parseFnDecl`
-- from desynchronising when a fn signature uses a parametric type.
-- Nested brackets like `Foo[Bar[Baz]]` are handled by recursing into
-- `parseSkipBrackType` on each inner `TUpper`.
fn parseSkipBrackType(toks List[Token]) =
  | TLBrack :: TUpper _ :: rest ->
    let rest2 = parseSkipBrackType rest in
    let rest3 = match rest2 with
      | TRBrack :: r -> r
      | _ -> rest2
    in
    parseSkipBrackType rest3
  | _ -> toks

-- Zero or more param groups. `()` produces no params; `(name Type)`
-- adds a `MkParam` to the running list. Phase 7.9d: the type after the
-- param name may now be a bracket-form parametric application, e.g.
-- `(xs List[Int])`. We still store only the head name in the AST
-- (via `TR tname`) and skip the bracket payload with
-- `parseSkipBrackType` so the outer decl loop resumes cleanly at the
-- closing `)`.
fn parseParamGroups(toks List[Token]) =
  | TLParen :: TRParen :: rest -> ([], rest)
  | TLParen :: TLower pname :: TUpper tname :: rest ->
    let rest2 = parseSkipBrackType rest in
    let rest3 = match rest2 with
      | TRParen :: r -> r
      | _ -> rest2
    in
    let (ps, rest4) = parseParamGroups rest3 in
    (MkParam pname (TR tname) :: ps, rest4)
  | _ -> ([], toks)

-- Optional return type: a `TUpper` immediately before `=` becomes
-- `Some (TR name)`, anything else stays `None`. Phase 7.9d: the head
-- `TUpper` may be followed by one or more `[ type ]` bracket groups
-- (e.g. `Maybe[Int]`, `Result[A][E]`). The brackets are consumed and
-- discarded — only the head name survives into `TR` — so the caller
-- can still find the trailing `=` and proceed into the body.
fn parseReturnType(toks List[Token]) =
  | TUpper tname :: rest ->
    let rest2 = parseSkipBrackType rest in
    (Some (TR tname), rest2)
  | _ -> (None, toks)

-- ---------- Expression parser (subset of 14-exprparser-real.lll) -------

-- Decide whether a token can start an atom. Used by parseApp to know
-- when to stop collecting arguments in a juxtaposition chain. Only
-- atom-starters here: TInt, TLower, TUpper, TLParen. Newlines and
-- keywords fall through to `false` so the application layer stops
-- cleanly at decl boundaries and before `then`/`else` tokens.
fn isAtomStart(t Token) Bool =
  | TInt _ -> true
  | TStr _ -> true
  | TChar _ -> true
  | TLower _ -> true
  | TUpper _ -> true
  | TLParen -> true
  | TLBrack -> true
  | _ -> false

-- Top-level expression dispatcher. Phase 7.5a added `match` as a second
-- special form alongside `if`. Phase 7.5b adds `let-in` (`TKwLet`) and
-- lambda (`TBackslash`) as two more special forms — both dispatched
-- here so they can nest freely inside any other expression. Note: the
-- top-level `parseDecls` has its own `TKwLet :: _` arm for module-level
-- `let name = expr` decls (no `in` required). At expression position,
-- the same token starts a let-in chain via `parseLetIn`. The two entry
-- points are independent so the context naturally disambiguates.
fn parseExpr(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwIf :: rest -> parseIf rest
  | TKwMatch :: rest -> parseMatch rest
  | TKwLet :: rest -> parseLetIn rest
  | TBackslash :: rest -> parseLam rest
  | _ -> parseCompare toks

-- `let name = e1 in e2`. Single-binder let-in. Chains are expressed by
-- nesting another `let ... in ...` in the `e2` slot (right-recursive).
-- Both `e1` and `e2` are parsed via the full `parseExpr` so any
-- expression form the fn-body grammar accepts (including nested lambdas
-- and further let-in chains) works on either side.
fn parseLetIn(toks List[Token]) =
  | TLower name :: TEq :: rest ->
    -- Phase 7.9.newlines (#8): `let x =\n  rhs` and `in\n  body` both
    -- need to tolerate a `TNewline` before their sub-expression, so
    -- we `skipNewlines` before each `parseExpr` call below.
    let rest0 = skipNewlines rest in
    let (e1, rest2) = parseExpr rest0 in
    let rest3 = match rest2 with
      | TKwIn :: r -> skipNewlines r
      | _ -> rest2
    in
    let (e2, rest4) = parseExpr rest3 in
    (ELetIn name e1 e2, rest4)
  | _ -> (EInt 0, toks)

-- `\x. body`. Single-param lambda; multi-param is expressed by nesting
-- (`\x. \y. body`). The body is parsed at full `parseExpr` level so it
-- can contain any expression form the grammar accepts.
fn parseLam(toks List[Token]) =
  | TLower name :: TDot :: rest ->
    let (body, rest2) = parseExpr rest in
    (ELam name body, rest2)
  | _ -> (EInt 0, toks)

-- `if c then a else b`. Each of the three sub-expressions is parsed via
-- the full parseExpr so nested `if`s work naturally. Phase 7.9q adds
-- newline tolerance around `then`/`else`: the bootstrap's own source
-- uses the multi-line form
--   if c < 65 then false
--   else if c > 90 then false
--   else true
-- which tokenises as `... TNewline TKwElse ...` between arms. Before
-- 7.9q the `TKwElse :: r` match arm missed the leading `TNewline` and
-- fell through, so `parseExpr` was called on `TNewline :: TKwElse :: ...`,
-- bottomed out in `parseAtom`'s wildcard as `(EInt 0, toks)`, and the
-- stray `TKwElse` was left on the stream — which then terminated
-- `parseDecls` at the next iteration and silently dropped every
-- remaining decl. Mirrors the host parser's `skipNewlines c` calls
-- around the same `KwThen`/`KwElse` sites in `Parser.fs`.
fn parseIf(toks List[Token]) =
  let (c, rest) = parseExpr toks in
  let rest1 = skipNewlines rest in
  let rest2 = match rest1 with
    | TKwThen :: r -> skipNewlines r
    | _ -> rest1
  in
  let (a, rest3) = parseExpr rest2 in
  let rest3a = skipNewlines rest3 in
  let rest4 = match rest3a with
    | TKwElse :: r -> skipNewlines r
    | _ -> rest3a
  in
  let (b, rest5) = parseExpr rest4 in
  (EIf c a b, rest5)

-- `match scrut with | pat -> body | pat -> body ...`. Same shape as the
-- helper in 14-exprparser-real.lll: scrutinee is parsed via `parseExpr`
-- (it stops cleanly at `TKwWith` because `with` is not an atom-starter
-- nor an arithmetic operator), then `parseArms` pulls the two parallel
-- arm lists out of the rest of the token stream.
fn parseMatch(toks List[Token]) =
  let (scrut, rest) = parseExpr toks in
  let rest2 = match rest with
    | TKwWith :: r -> r
    | _ -> rest
  in
  let (armLists, rest3) = parseArms rest2 in
  let (pats, bodies) = armLists in
  (EMatch scrut pats bodies, rest3)

-- Collect arms while the next token is `|`. Returns `((pats, bodies),
-- rest)` — the two arm lists stay in lockstep because each recursive
-- step cons'es onto both at once. The outer result is still a 2-tuple
-- `(result, leftover)` like every other parseX helper.
fn parseArms(toks List[Token]) =
  -- Phase 7.9.newlines (#12): a multi-line match body has `TNewline`
  -- tokens between arms (and before the first arm), so peek for the
  -- leading `TBar` only AFTER skipping newlines. Otherwise the first
  -- `TNewline :: TBar :: _` falls through to the `(([], []), toks)`
  -- branch and silently drops every subsequent arm.
  let toks2 = skipNewlines toks in
  match toks2 with
    | TBar :: _ ->
      let (armPair, rest) = parseArm toks2 in
      let (p, b) = armPair in
      let (moreLists, rest2) = parseArms rest in
      let (ps, bs) = moreLists in
      ((p :: ps, b :: bs), rest2)
    | _ -> (([], []), toks2)

-- Single arm. Assumes the leading `|` is still on the front. Returns
-- `((pat, body), leftover)` so the caller can destructure cleanly.
fn parseArm(toks List[Token]) =
  | TBar :: rest ->
    let (p, rest2) = parsePat rest in
    let rest3 = match rest2 with
      | TArrow :: r -> r
      | _ -> rest2
    in
    let (body, rest4) = parseArmBody rest3 in
    ((p, body), rest4)
  | _ -> ((PWild, EInt 0), toks)

-- Arm bodies stop at the next `|` (start of sibling arm) or at TNewline
-- / TEnd (decl boundary). Parsing at the `parseCompare` level rather
-- than full `parseExpr` prevents an arm body from accidentally grabbing
-- a trailing `| ...` as part of a nested match. Phase 7.10b: the
-- bootstrap's own source now uses `if ... then ... else ...` and
-- `let x = ... in ...` directly inside arm bodies (e.g. `takeIdCont`:
-- `| c :: rest -> if isIdCont c then listAppend [c] (takeIdCont rest)
-- else []`), so we dispatch `TKwIf` / `TKwLet` explicitly at the top
-- and fall through to `parseCompare` otherwise. `TKwMatch` and
-- `TBackslash` are deliberately NOT dispatched here: a nested `match`
-- inside an arm body is still the ambiguous case the original
-- constraint existed to guard against, and the bootstrap never puts a
-- bare lambda in arm-body position.
fn parseArmBody(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwIf :: rest -> parseIf rest
  | TKwLet :: rest -> parseLetIn rest
  | _ -> parseCompare toks

-- Phase 7.9l: eagerly consume a sequence of atomic sub-patterns (var
-- binder, wildcard, int literal, or empty-list `[]`) until the next
-- token doesn't start a pattern (like `TArrow` / `TBar` /
-- `TColonColon` / `TRParen`). Deliberately does NOT recurse into
-- nested `TUpper` constructors without parentheses — so a nested
-- `Cons (Some 1) None` needs parens for the `Some 1`, but the
-- bootstrap rarely needs nested ctors without parens.
fn parsePatArgs(toks List[Token]) =
  match toks with
    | TLower _ :: _ -> parsePatArgsCons toks
    | TUnder :: _ -> parsePatArgsCons toks
    | TInt _ :: _ -> parsePatArgsCons toks
    | TLBrack :: TRBrack :: _ -> parsePatArgsCons toks
    | _ -> ([], toks)

fn parsePatArgsCons(toks List[Token]) =
  let (p, rest) = parsePrimaryPat toks in
  let (ps, rest2) = parsePatArgs rest in
  (listAppend [p] ps, rest2)

-- Phase 7.9l: fold a `TUpper name :: rest` constructor pattern into a
-- `PCon name args` node. Consumes the ctor name plus any trailing
-- atomic sub-patterns via `parsePatArgs`.
fn parseCtorArgs(name Str)(toks List[Token]) =
  let (args, rest) = parsePatArgs toks in
  (PCon name args, rest)

-- Primary pattern: int literal, lowercase var binder, wildcard `_`,
-- empty-list `[]`, or `TUpper`-headed constructor pattern. The
-- recursive `parsePat` calls this once, then peeks for `TColonColon`
-- to optionally fold the result into a right-associative `PCons`.
-- Phase 7.9l added the `TUpper name :: rest` arm for constructor
-- patterns. List-literal / tuple / string patterns all stay in
-- Phase 7.5+.
fn parsePrimaryPat(toks List[Token]) =
  | TInt n :: rest -> (PInt n, rest)
  | TStr s :: rest -> (PStr s, rest)
  | TUnder :: rest -> (PWild, rest)
  | TLBrack :: TRBrack :: rest -> (PNil, rest)
  | TLower s :: rest -> (PVar s, rest)
  | TUpper name :: rest -> parseCtorArgs name rest
  | _ -> (PWild, toks)

-- Pattern: a primary pattern optionally followed by `:: pat`. The
-- right side is parsed via `parsePat` itself so chains like
-- `a :: b :: rest` fold right-associatively into `PCons a (PCons b
-- (PVar "rest"))`. Phase 7.5c added `PNil` and `PCons`.
fn parsePat(toks List[Token]) =
  let (p, rest) = parsePrimaryPat toks in
  match rest with
    | TColonColon :: rest2 ->
      let (tail, rest3) = parsePat rest2 in
      (PCons p tail, rest3)
    | _ -> (p, rest)

-- Phase 7.9f: comparison precedence layer. Looser than `+/-`, so the
-- cascade goes parseExpr -> parseCompare -> parseAddSub -> parseMulDiv
-- -> parseApp -> parseAtom. Only `==` (TEqEq) in this slice; `<`, `>`,
-- `!=` stay in Phase 7.9g+. Left-associative via the tail helper, same
-- shape as parseAddSubTail.
fn parseCompare(toks List[Token]) =
  let (e, rest) = parseAddSub toks in
  parseCompareTail e rest

fn parseCompareTail(lhs Expr)(toks List[Token]) =
  match toks with
    | TEqEq :: r ->
      let (rhs, rest2) = parseAddSub r in
      parseCompareTail (EEq lhs rhs) rest2
    | TLt :: r ->
      let (rhs, rest2) = parseAddSub r in
      parseCompareTail (ELt lhs rhs) rest2
    | TGt :: r ->
      let (rhs, rest2) = parseAddSub r in
      parseCompareTail (EGt lhs rhs) rest2
    | _ -> (lhs, toks)

-- Arithmetic precedence cascade, same shape as 13/14.
fn parseAddSub(toks List[Token]) =
  let (e, rest) = parseMulDiv toks in
  parseAddSubTail e rest

fn parseAddSubTail(lhs Expr)(toks List[Token]) =
  | TPlus :: rest ->
    let (r, rest2) = parseMulDiv rest in
    parseAddSubTail (EAdd lhs r) rest2
  | TMinus :: rest ->
    let (r, rest2) = parseMulDiv rest in
    parseAddSubTail (ESub lhs r) rest2
  | _ -> (lhs, toks)

fn parseMulDiv(toks List[Token]) =
  let (e, rest) = parseApp toks in
  parseMulDivTail e rest

fn parseMulDivTail(lhs Expr)(toks List[Token]) =
  | TStar :: rest ->
    let (r, rest2) = parseApp rest in
    parseMulDivTail (EMul lhs r) rest2
  | TSlash :: rest ->
    let (r, rest2) = parseApp rest in
    parseMulDivTail (EDiv lhs r) rest2
  | _ -> (lhs, toks)

-- Application layer: left-associative juxtaposition. Collects atom args
-- while the head token is an atom-starter.
fn parseApp(toks List[Token]) =
  let (e, rest) = parseAtom toks in
  parseAppTail e rest

fn parseAppTail(lhs Expr)(toks List[Token]) =
  match toks with
    | t :: _ ->
      if isAtomStart t then
        let (arg, rest) = parseAtom toks in
        parseAppTail (EApp lhs arg) rest
      else (lhs, toks)
    | _ -> (lhs, toks)

-- Atom: int, string literal, lower / upper variable reference,
-- parenthesised expr. Anything unexpected falls through to a defensive
-- `EInt 0`. Phase 7.5c added the `TStr s :: rest -> (EStr s, rest)`
-- arm; the lexer already produces `TStr` for `"..."` literals. Phase
-- 7.5d folds a trailing `[Tag]` into `ETagged` by dispatching on the
-- wider 4-token prefix `TInt/TStr :: TLBrack :: TUpper ty :: TRBrack
-- :: rest` BEFORE falling through to the plain 1-token `TInt n :: rest`
-- / `TStr s :: rest` arms. Mirrors the host compiler's Phase 7.2.2
-- rule: only int / str literal atoms can take a tag — other atom
-- shapes (`TLower`, `TUpper`, `TLParen`) never try to match a `[Tag]`
-- suffix here, so a stray `[` in those positions stays on the token
-- stream and would later error out.
fn parseAtom(toks List[Token]) =
  | TInt n :: TLBrack :: TUpper ty :: TRBrack :: rest -> (ETagged (EInt n) ty, rest)
  | TStr s :: TLBrack :: TUpper ty :: TRBrack :: rest -> (ETagged (EStr s) ty, rest)
  | TInt n :: rest -> (EInt n, rest)
  | TStr s :: rest -> (EStr s, rest)
  | TChar c :: rest -> (EChar c, rest)
  | TLower s :: rest -> (EVar s, rest)
  | TUpper s :: rest -> (EVar s, rest)
  | TLBrack :: TRBrack :: rest -> (ENil, rest)
  | TLBrack :: rest -> parseListLit rest
  | TLParen :: rest ->
    let (e, rest2) = parseExpr rest in
    match rest2 with
      | TRParen :: rest3 -> (e, rest3)
      | _ -> (e, rest2)
  | _ -> (EInt 0, toks)

-- Phase 7.10a: parse a list literal body (after `[` is consumed).
-- Reads atoms until `TRBrack`, folding right-associatively into
-- `ECons`. Elements are parsed via `parseAtom` (not `parseExpr`) —
-- the bootstrap source only uses simple list literals like `[c]` /
-- `[1 2 3]` / `[TKwModule]` where every element is a var / literal /
-- ctor atom. A complex expression element would need `parseExpr` and
-- an escalation to Phase 7.10b.
fn parseListLit(toks List[Token]) =
  let (e, rest) = parseAtom toks in
  match rest with
    | TRBrack :: rest2 -> (ECons e ENil, rest2)
    | _ ->
      let (tail, rest3) = parseListLit rest in
      (ECons e tail, rest3)

-- ---------- Pretty printer ----------------------------------------------

-- Render a TypeArg as its bare name; type vars and type cons are
-- disambiguated by the wrapping context. Phase 7.5d: `TAApp head args`
-- renders as `head[arg1][arg2]...` — each bracket group is emitted
-- via the `showBrackArgs` helper, which recursively calls `showTypeArg`
-- on every inner arg so nested applications `Foo[Bar[Baz]]` print
-- uniformly.
fn showTypeArg(a TypeArg) Str =
  | TAVar s -> s
  | TACon s -> s
  | TAApp head args -> strConcat head (showBrackArgs args)

-- Render a list of bracket-form args as `[a1][a2]...`. One bracket
-- group per arg, matching the surface input shape `Result[A][E]`. Lives
-- separately from `showArgs` (which renders comma-joined parens for
-- ctor-arg lists).
fn showBrackArgs(args List[TypeArg]) Str =
  listFold (\acc a. strConcat (strConcat acc "[") (strConcat (showTypeArg a) "]")) "" args

-- Render a list of args as `(a1, a2)` or empty string for no args.
fn showArgs(args List[TypeArg]) Str =
  if listIsEmpty args then ""
  else
    let inner = listFold (\acc a. if strLen acc == 0 then showTypeArg a else strConcat (strConcat acc ", ") (showTypeArg a)) "" args in
    strConcat (strConcat "(" inner) ")"

fn showCtor(c Ctor) Str =
  | MkCtor name args -> strConcat name (showArgs args)

fn showCtors(cs List[Ctor]) Str =
  listFold (\acc c. if strLen acc == 0 then showCtor c else strConcat (strConcat acc " | ") (showCtor c)) "" cs

-- Render the type-param list as ` (A) (B)` with a leading space, or empty.
fn showTypeParams(ps List[Str]) Str =
  listFold (\acc p. strConcat (strConcat acc " (") (strConcat p ")")) "" ps

fn showTypeDecl(d TypeDecl) Str =
  | MkTypeDecl name prms ctors ->
    let head = strConcat (strConcat "type " name) (showTypeParams prms) in
    strConcat (strConcat head " = ") (showCtors ctors)

-- Fn-decl pretty printer, matching the shape used by 13-fnparser-real.lll.
fn showTypeRef(t TypeRef) Str =
  | TR s -> s

fn showParam(p Param) Str =
  | MkParam n t -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" n) ": ") (strConcat (showTypeRef t) ")")

fn showFnParams(ps List[Param]) Str =
  if listIsEmpty ps then " ()"
  else listFold (\acc p. strConcat (strConcat acc " ") (showParam p)) "" ps

fn showReturn(r Maybe[TypeRef]) Str =
  match r with
    | Some t -> showTypeRef t
    | None -> "?"

-- Pattern pretty-printer for match arms (Phase 7.5a + 7.5c + 7.9l).
-- Phase 7.5c added `PNil` (rendered as `[]`) and `PCons h t`
-- (rendered as `(h :: t)`, recursively, so chains nest as
-- `(a :: (b :: rest))`). Phase 7.9l added `PCon name args` (rendered
-- as `(Name arg1 arg2)`, a space before each arg).
fn showPat(p Pat) Str =
  | PInt n -> intToStr n
  | PStr s -> strConcat (strConcat "\"" s) "\""
  | PVar s -> s
  | PWild -> "_"
  | PNil -> "[]"
  | PCons h t -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showPat h)) " :: ") (showPat t)) ")"
  | PCon name args -> strConcat "(" (strConcat name (strConcat (showPatArgs args) ")"))

fn showPatArgs(ps List[Pat]) Str =
  match ps with
    | [] -> ""
    | p :: rest -> strConcat " " (strConcat (showPat p) (showPatArgs rest))

-- Render a single `| pat -> body` arm; helper for `showArms`.
fn showArm(p Pat)(body Expr) Str =
  strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "| " (showPat p)) " -> ") (showExpr body)

-- Walk the two parallel lists in lockstep to produce `| pat -> body`
-- chunks joined by single spaces. The invariant `length pats == length
-- bodies` is maintained by `parseArms`.
fn showArms(pats List[Pat])(bodies List[Expr]) Str =
  match pats with
    | p :: ps ->
      match bodies with
        | b :: bs ->
          let head = showArm p b in
          let tail = showArms ps bs in
          if strLen tail == 0 then head
          else strConcat (strConcat head " ") tail
        | _ -> ""
    | _ -> ""

-- Fully-parenthesised expression pretty-printer, matching 13/14's shape
-- so the runtime test can substring-match each body.
fn showExpr(e Expr) Str =
  | EInt n -> intToStr n
  | EStr s -> strConcat (strConcat "\"" s) "\""
  | EChar c -> strConcat (strConcat "'" (strFromChars [c])) "'"
  | EVar s -> s
  | ETagged inner t -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showExpr inner)) "[") t) "])"
  | EAdd l r -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showExpr l)) " + ") (showExpr r)) ")"
  | ESub l r -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showExpr l)) " - ") (showExpr r)) ")"
  | EMul l r -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showExpr l)) " * ") (showExpr r)) ")"
  | EDiv l r -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showExpr l)) " / ") (showExpr r)) ")"
  | EEq l r -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showExpr l)) " == ") (showExpr r)) ")"
  | ELt l r -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showExpr l)) " < ") (showExpr r)) ")"
  | EGt l r -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showExpr l)) " > ") (showExpr r)) ")"
  | EApp f x -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showExpr f)) " ") (showExpr x)) ")"
  | ELam n body -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(fun " n) " -> ") (showExpr body)) ")"
  | ELetIn n e1 e2 ->
    let p1 = strConcat (strConcat "(let " n) " = " in
    let p2 = strConcat (strConcat p1 (showExpr e1)) " in " in
    strConcat (strConcat p2 (showExpr e2)) ")"
  | EIf c a b ->
    let p1 = strConcat "(if " (showExpr c) in
    let p2 = strConcat (strConcat p1 " then ") (showExpr a) in
    strConcat (strConcat (strConcat p2 " else ") (showExpr b)) ")"
  | EMatch scrut pats bodies ->
    let head = strConcat (strConcat "(match " (showExpr scrut)) " with " in
    strConcat (strConcat head (showArms pats bodies)) ")"
  | ENil -> "[]"
  | ECons h t -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showExpr h)) " :: ") (showExpr t)) ")"

fn showFnDecl(d FnDecl) Str =
  | MkFn name prms ret body ->
    let headPart = strConcat (strConcat "fn " name) (showFnParams prms) in
    let arrowPart = strConcat (strConcat headPart " -> ") (showReturn ret) in
    strConcat (strConcat arrowPart " = ") (showExpr body)

-- Let-decl pretty printer (Phase 7.5a): `let <name> = <show expr>`.
fn showLetDecl(d LetDecl) Str =
  | MkLet name body ->
    strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "let " name) " = ") (showExpr body)

-- Phase 7.5e: `DTag` / `DImport` / `DExport` pretty-printer arms. The
-- `DExport inner` wrapper recurses back into `showDecl` so the inner
-- decl's canonical pretty form stays untouched and the `export `
-- modifier stacks in front of any shape — `export fn ...`, `export
-- type ...`, `export let ...`, even `export tag ...` / `export import
-- ...` if the grammar ever wants it.
fn showDecl(d Decl) Str =
  | DType td -> showTypeDecl td
  | DLet ld -> showLetDecl ld
  | DFn fd -> showFnDecl fd
  | DTag name -> strConcat "tag " name
  | DImport path -> strConcat "import " path
  | DExport inner -> strConcat "export " (showDecl inner)

-- Render a list of decls joined by `\n`, matching the input layout.
fn showDecls(ds List[Decl]) Str =
  listFold (\acc d. if strLen acc == 0 then showDecl d else strConcat (strConcat acc "\n") (showDecl d)) "" ds

fn showModule(m Module) Str =
  | MkModule name decls ->
    let header = strConcat "module " name in
    if listIsEmpty decls then header
    else strConcat (strConcat header "\n") (showDecls decls)

-- ---------- Elaborator (Phase 7.6 integration) --------------------------
--
-- Name-resolution env: just a `List[Str]` of declared names. No types,
-- no source positions — this slice cares only about "is this name
-- defined?". Wrapped in `MkEnv` so future slices can carry richer data
-- (per-name types, scope depth, etc.) without changing the surface.
type Env = MkEnv List[Str]

fn envHas(env Env)(name Str) Bool =
  match env with
    | MkEnv xs ->
      listFold (\acc x. if acc then true else if x == name then true else false) false xs

fn envAdd(env Env)(name Str) Env =
  match env with
    | MkEnv xs -> MkEnv (listAppend xs [name])

-- Fold a list of names into an env, extending one binder at a time.
fn envAddAll(env Env)(names List[Str]) Env =
  listFold (\acc n. envAdd acc n) env names

-- Collect every `PVar` binder introduced by a pattern. Wildcards, nil,
-- int-literal patterns don't bind anything. `PCons h t` binds whatever
-- the head and tail patterns bind, left-to-right. Phase 7.9l added
-- `PCon name args` which binds whatever its arg patterns bind, in
-- left-to-right order via `patBindersList`.
fn patBinders(p Pat) List[Str] =
  | PInt _ -> []
  | PStr _ -> []
  | PWild -> []
  | PNil -> []
  | PVar name -> [name]
  | PCons h t -> listAppend (patBinders h) (patBinders t)
  | PCon _ args -> patBindersList args

fn patBindersList(ps List[Pat]) List[Str] =
  match ps with
    | [] -> []
    | p :: rest -> listAppend (patBinders p) (patBindersList rest)

-- Extract the param name from a `MkParam name (TR ty)`. Used when a
-- `DFn` extends the local env for its body walk.
fn paramName(p Param) Str =
  | MkParam n _ -> n

fn paramNames(ps List[Param]) List[Str] =
  listFold (\acc p. listAppend acc [(paramName p)]) [] ps

-- Walk `e` under `env`, returning the list of `E002 UnboundVar <name>`
-- error strings produced by every `EVar name` not in `env` plus any
-- errors produced by sub-expressions. The only arms that actually
-- flag are `EVar`; every compound arm recurses and concatenates its
-- children's error lists. `ELetIn` / `ELam` / `EMatch` extend the
-- local env for their body sub-expression(s) before recursing.
fn checkExpr(env Env)(e Expr) List[Str] =
  | EInt _ -> []
  | EStr _ -> []
  | EChar _ -> []
  | EVar name ->
    if envHas env name then []
    else
      let msg = strConcat "E002 UnboundVar " name in
      [msg]
  | ETagged inner _ -> checkExpr env inner
  | EAdd l r -> listAppend (checkExpr env l) (checkExpr env r)
  | ESub l r -> listAppend (checkExpr env l) (checkExpr env r)
  | EMul l r -> listAppend (checkExpr env l) (checkExpr env r)
  | EDiv l r -> listAppend (checkExpr env l) (checkExpr env r)
  | EEq l r -> listAppend (checkExpr env l) (checkExpr env r)
  | ELt l r -> listAppend (checkExpr env l) (checkExpr env r)
  | EGt l r -> listAppend (checkExpr env l) (checkExpr env r)
  | EApp f x -> listAppend (checkExpr env f) (checkExpr env x)
  | ELam param body ->
    let env2 = envAdd env param in
    checkExpr env2 body
  | ELetIn name rhs body ->
    let rhsErrs = checkExpr env rhs in
    let env2 = envAdd env name in
    let bodyErrs = checkExpr env2 body in
    listAppend rhsErrs bodyErrs
  | EIf cnd thn els ->
    let ce = checkExpr env cnd in
    let te = checkExpr env thn in
    let ee = checkExpr env els in
    listAppend (listAppend ce te) ee
  | EMatch scrut pats bodies ->
    let scrutErrs = checkExpr env scrut in
    let armErrs = checkArms env pats bodies in
    listAppend scrutErrs armErrs
  | ENil -> []
  | ECons h t -> listAppend (checkExpr env h) (checkExpr env t)

-- Walk the two parallel arm lists in lockstep. For each arm, extend
-- the scrutinee env with every binder the pattern introduces, then
-- walk the body expression under the extended env. Pattern binders
-- are only visible inside their own arm body, not across arms.
fn checkArms(env Env)(pats List[Pat])(bodies List[Expr]) List[Str] =
  match pats with
    | p :: ps ->
      match bodies with
        | b :: bs ->
          let armEnv = envAddAll env (patBinders p) in
          let armErrs = checkExpr armEnv b in
          let restErrs = checkArms env ps bs in
          listAppend armErrs restErrs
        | _ -> []
    | _ -> []

-- Walk a single decl under the top-level `env`. `DFn` extends `env`
-- with its own params (fn params are locals, not top-level names)
-- then walks `body`. `DLet`'s RHS is also walked — unlike 16 which
-- skipped it. `DType` / `DTag` / `DImport` have no sub-expressions.
-- `DExport inner` recurses into the inner decl so an exported fn's
-- body still gets name-resolved.
fn checkFnBody(env Env)(fd FnDecl) List[Str] =
  | MkFn _ params _ body ->
    let bodyEnv = envAddAll env (paramNames params) in
    checkExpr bodyEnv body

fn checkLetBody(env Env)(ld LetDecl) List[Str] =
  | MkLet _ body -> checkExpr env body

fn checkDecl(env Env)(d Decl) List[Str] =
  | DFn fd -> checkFnBody env fd
  | DLet ld -> checkLetBody env ld
  | DType _ -> []
  | DTag _ -> []
  | DImport _ -> []
  | DExport inner -> checkDecl env inner

fn checkDecls(env Env)(decls List[Decl]) List[Str] =
  | d :: rest -> listAppend (checkDecl env d) (checkDecls env rest)
  | _ -> []

-- Collect the single top-level name each decl introduces into the env.
-- `DFn` / `DLet` / `DTag` each contribute one name; `DType` contributes
-- every ctor name (the type name itself isn't a value, matching the F#
-- host elaborator). `DImport` contributes nothing in this slice (real
-- module resolution is a later phase). `DExport inner` recurses into
-- the inner decl so `export fn foo` still registers `foo`.
fn ctorName(c Ctor) Str =
  | MkCtor n _ -> n

fn ctorNames(cs List[Ctor]) List[Str] =
  listFold (\acc c. listAppend acc [(ctorName c)]) [] cs

fn fnName(fd FnDecl) Str =
  | MkFn n _ _ _ -> n

fn letName(ld LetDecl) Str =
  | MkLet n _ -> n

fn typeDeclCtors(td TypeDecl) List[Str] =
  | MkTypeDecl _ _ ctors -> ctorNames ctors

fn collectDecl(env Env)(d Decl) Env =
  | DFn fd -> envAdd env (fnName fd)
  | DLet ld -> envAdd env (letName ld)
  | DType td -> envAddAll env (typeDeclCtors td)
  | DTag name -> envAdd env name
  | DImport _ -> env
  | DExport inner -> collectDecl env inner

fn collectDecls(env Env)(decls List[Decl]) Env =
  | d :: rest -> collectDecls (collectDecl env d) rest
  | _ -> env

-- Type -> ctor index. Build a `List[TypeCtors]` indexed by type name
-- so the exhaustiveness check can answer "what ctors does type T
-- have?". Only `DType` decls contribute; `DExport (DType td)` is
-- unwrapped one level so exported types still register their ctors.
type TypeCtors = MkTypeCtors Str List[Str]

fn typeDeclName(td TypeDecl) Str =
  | MkTypeDecl n _ _ -> n

fn collectTypesDecl(d Decl) List[TypeCtors] =
  | DType td -> [(MkTypeCtors (typeDeclName td) (typeDeclCtors td))]
  | DExport inner -> collectTypesDecl inner
  | _ -> []

fn collectTypes(decls List[Decl]) List[TypeCtors] =
  | d :: rest -> listAppend (collectTypesDecl d) (collectTypes rest)
  | _ -> []

-- Linear lookup: returns the ctor list for the given type name, or
-- the empty list if the name isn't a declared sum type.
fn lookupCtors(types List[TypeCtors])(name Str) List[Str] =
  match types with
    | MkTypeCtors n cs :: rest ->
      if n == name then cs else lookupCtors rest name
    | _ -> []

-- A pattern counts as "catch-all relative to a sum type" if it's
-- `PWild`, `PVar`, or `PCons` — mirrors the F# host elaborator's
-- catch-all list. `PInt` / `PNil` / `PCon` are NOT catch-alls —
-- `PCon` matches only its own tag.
fn patIsCatchAll(p Pat) Bool =
  | PWild -> true
  | PVar _ -> true
  | PCons _ _ -> true
  | PCon _ _ -> false
  | PStr _ -> false
  | _ -> false

fn armsHaveCatchAll(pats List[Pat]) Bool =
  listFold (\acc p. if acc then true else patIsCatchAll p) false pats

-- Phase 7.9l: now that `Pat` has `PCon name args`, a match arm can
-- identify a sum-type ctor. Walk the arm pattern list and collect the
-- name from every `PCon`. Non-ctor patterns (`PWild` / `PVar` /
-- `PInt` / `PNil` / `PCons`) contribute nothing — the catch-all /
-- cons / literal cases are handled elsewhere (`armsHaveCatchAll` /
-- coverage logic is disabled when a catch-all is present).
fn coveredCtors(pats List[Pat]) List[Str] =
  match pats with
    | p :: rest ->
      let tail = coveredCtors rest in
      match p with
        | PCon name _ -> listAppend [name] tail
        | _ -> tail
    | _ -> []

-- For a given `required` ctor list and `covered` ctor list, return
-- one `E003 NonExhaustiveMatch <type> missing <ctor>` message per
-- required ctor that isn't in `covered`.
fn missingCtorErrs(ty Str)(required List[Str])(covered List[Str]) List[Str] =
  match required with
    | c :: rest ->
      let tail = missingCtorErrs ty rest covered in
      let isCovered = listFold (\acc x. if acc then true else if x == c then true else false) false covered in
      if isCovered then tail
      else
        let msg = strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "E003 NonExhaustiveMatch " ty) " missing ") c in
        listAppend [msg] tail
    | _ -> []

-- Extract the type name out of a param (via `MkParam _ (TR t)`).
-- `paramTypeName` returns just the string.
fn typeRefName(tr TypeRef) Str =
  | TR s -> s

fn paramTypeName(p Param) Str =
  | MkParam _ tr -> typeRefName tr

-- Extract the last param's type name from a param list. Returns "" if
-- the list is empty. Folds left so the final non-"" name wins —
-- mirrors the F# host elaborator's "clause sugar scrutinizes the
-- LAST curried param" rule.
fn lastParamTypeName(ps List[Param]) Str =
  listFold (\acc p. paramTypeName p) "" ps

-- Run the exhaustiveness check on a single `FnDecl` body. Shared
-- between the plain `DFn fd` arm and the `DExport (DFn fd)` unwrap.
fn exhaustivenessFn(types List[TypeCtors])(fd FnDecl) List[Str] =
  | MkFn _ params _ body ->
    let lastTy = lastParamTypeName params in
    if strLen lastTy == 0 then []
    else
      let required = lookupCtors types lastTy in
      if listIsEmpty required then []
      else
        match body with
          | EMatch _ pats _ ->
            if armsHaveCatchAll pats then []
            else
              let covered = coveredCtors pats in
              missingCtorErrs lastTy required covered
          | _ -> []

-- Run the exhaustiveness check over a single decl. Non-`DFn` decls
-- contribute nothing. `DExport (DFn ...)` is unwrapped one level.
fn exhaustivenessDecl(types List[TypeCtors])(d Decl) List[Str] =
  | DFn fd -> exhaustivenessFn types fd
  | DExport inner -> exhaustivenessDecl types inner
  | _ -> []

fn exhaustivenessCheck(types List[TypeCtors])(decls List[Decl]) List[Str] =
  | d :: rest ->
    listAppend (exhaustivenessDecl types d) (exhaustivenessCheck types rest)
  | _ -> []

-- ---------- Minimal HM pass (Phase 7.9a) ---------------------------------
--
-- Tight, deliberately-simple type checker for the parser's `Expr` AST.
-- The goal is to exercise the three-stage (parser + elaborator + HM)
-- integration on a single shared AST — NOT to reproduce
-- 18-hminfer-real.lll's full unify / Subst / fresh-var machinery.
--
-- Scope of the pass:
--   * `EInt _`           -> TyName "Int"
--   * `EStr _`           -> TyName "Str"
--   * `EVar name`        -> lookup in TypeEnv; missing names fall through
--                           to `TyVar "?"` (the elaborator already
--                           reported E002 for them)
--   * `EAdd/ESub/EMul/EDiv l r` -> TyName "Int"; emits E001 if either
--                           operand is a concrete non-Int type
--   * `EIf c t e`        -> inferred type of the `then` branch;
--                           emits E001 if `then` and `else` branches
--                           have concrete non-matching types
--   * everything else (`ELam`, `ELetIn`, `EMatch`, `ETagged`, `EApp`)
--     -> walked recursively so sub-expression errors still surface,
--        but the outer shape infers to `TyVar "?"` (wildcard).
--
-- `typeEq` treats `TyVar "?"` as a wildcard that matches anything,
-- mirroring the host elaborator's `tyEqual`. This keeps the pass
-- silent on unknown sub-expressions (no false positives) while still
-- firing on the concrete `Int` vs `Str` mismatches the test needs.
--
-- NOT in this slice (punted to Phase 7.9b integration):
--   * unify / Subst / fresh vars (see 18-hminfer-real.lll)
--   * let-generalization
--   * pattern-type checking
--   * constructor-arity checking

type TypeExpr =
  | TyName Str
  | TyVar Str

-- Per-name type binding for the HM pass. Only fn params get bindings
-- in this slice — the HM env is built fresh per fn body, seeded from
-- `MkParam name (TR tyName)`.
type TyBinding = MkTyBinding Str TypeExpr

type TypeEnv = MkTypeEnv List[TyBinding]

fn typeEnvAdd(env TypeEnv)(name Str)(ty TypeExpr) TypeEnv =
  match env with
    | MkTypeEnv bs -> MkTypeEnv (listAppend bs [(MkTyBinding name ty)])

-- Linear lookup. Returns `TyVar "?"` (the sentinel wildcard) when the
-- name isn't bound — every `typeEq` comparison against `TyVar "?"`
-- short-circuits to `true`, so unbound names never trigger E001.
fn typeEnvLookup(env TypeEnv)(name Str) TypeExpr =
  match env with
    | MkTypeEnv bs -> typeEnvLookupList bs name

fn typeEnvLookupList(bs List[TyBinding])(name Str) TypeExpr =
  match bs with
    | MkTyBinding n t :: rest ->
      if n == name then t else typeEnvLookupList rest name
    | _ -> TyVar "?"

-- Render a TypeExpr in a human-readable surface form. `TyName n` just
-- prints the name, `TyVar v` prints the raw var name (the `"?"`
-- wildcard would never actually hit an E001 message because `typeEq`
-- short-circuits on it, but the fallthrough is kept for defensive
-- debugging).
fn showType(t TypeExpr) Str =
  | TyName n -> n
  | TyVar v -> v

-- Structural equality on the two-variant `TypeExpr`. `TyVar "?"` on
-- either side is a wildcard that matches anything — this is the only
-- way unknown types (EApp / ELam results) get to silently slip past
-- the arithmetic / if checks. `TyVar` with any other name compares by
-- variable name (treated as a rigid var in this slice, unused so far).
fn typeEq(a TypeExpr)(b TypeExpr) Bool =
  match a with
    | TyVar va ->
      if va == "?" then true
      else
        match b with
          | TyVar vb -> va == vb
          | _ -> false
    | TyName na ->
      match b with
        | TyName nb -> na == nb
        | TyVar vb -> vb == "?"
        | _ -> false

-- Infer the type of a single expression under `env`. No unification
-- and no substitution — each arm just returns the obvious "surface"
-- type. Unknown / punted shapes return `TyVar "?"` so downstream
-- `typeEq` checks silently accept them.
fn inferExprType(env TypeEnv)(e Expr) TypeExpr =
  | EInt _ -> TyName "Int"
  | EStr _ -> TyName "Str"
  | EChar _ -> TyName "Char"
  | EVar name -> typeEnvLookup env name
  | ETagged inner _ -> inferExprType env inner
  | EAdd _ _ -> TyName "Int"
  | ESub _ _ -> TyName "Int"
  | EMul _ _ -> TyName "Int"
  | EDiv _ _ -> TyName "Int"
  | EEq _ _ -> TyName "Bool"
  | ELt _ _ -> TyName "Bool"
  | EGt _ _ -> TyName "Bool"
  | EIf _ thn _ -> inferExprType env thn
  | ENil -> TyName "List"
  | ECons _ _ -> TyName "List"
  | _ -> TyVar "?"

-- Emit a single `E001 TypeMismatch <l> vs <r>` string built from the
-- two `TypeExpr` operands' `showType` renderings. Shared helper so the
-- arithmetic arms and the `if` arm produce identically-shaped messages.
fn mkTypeMismatch(tl TypeExpr)(tr TypeExpr) Str =
  let head = strConcat "E001 TypeMismatch " (showType tl) in
  strConcat (strConcat head " vs ") (showType tr)

-- Check that both operands of a `+ - * /` expression infer to `Int`.
-- Any operand whose inferred type is NOT `typeEq` to `TyName "Int"`
-- contributes one E001 message. Inferred `TyVar "?"` short-circuits
-- (via typeEq) to silent — so `EVar` references to unbound names and
-- `EApp` results never produce false positives.
fn checkArith(env TypeEnv)(l Expr)(r Expr) List[Str] =
  let tl = inferExprType env l in
  let tr = inferExprType env r in
  let tyInt = TyName "Int" in
  let lErr = if typeEq tl tyInt then [] else [(mkTypeMismatch tyInt tl)] in
  let rErr = if typeEq tr tyInt then [] else [(mkTypeMismatch tyInt tr)] in
  listAppend lErr rErr

-- Check that the `then` / `else` branches of an `if` expression agree
-- on type. One E001 per mismatched pair; silent when either branch
-- infers to `TyVar "?"`.
fn checkIfBranches(env TypeEnv)(thn Expr)(els Expr) List[Str] =
  let tt = inferExprType env thn in
  let te = inferExprType env els in
  if typeEq tt te then []
  else [(mkTypeMismatch tt te)]

-- Walk an expression under `env`, returning the list of E001 type
-- mismatch errors produced at every arithmetic or if-branch node. The
-- recursive walker short-circuits punted shapes (`ELam`, `ELetIn`,
-- `EMatch`) by simply recursing into their sub-expressions — the
-- punted shapes don't themselves emit errors, only their children
-- might. `EMatch` walks the scrutinee and every arm body.
fn typeCheck(env TypeEnv)(e Expr) List[Str] =
  | EInt _ -> []
  | EStr _ -> []
  | EChar _ -> []
  | EVar _ -> []
  | ETagged inner _ -> typeCheck env inner
  | EAdd l r ->
    let here = checkArith env l r in
    let lErrs = typeCheck env l in
    let rErrs = typeCheck env r in
    listAppend here (listAppend lErrs rErrs)
  | ESub l r ->
    let here = checkArith env l r in
    let lErrs = typeCheck env l in
    let rErrs = typeCheck env r in
    listAppend here (listAppend lErrs rErrs)
  | EMul l r ->
    let here = checkArith env l r in
    let lErrs = typeCheck env l in
    let rErrs = typeCheck env r in
    listAppend here (listAppend lErrs rErrs)
  | EDiv l r ->
    let here = checkArith env l r in
    let lErrs = typeCheck env l in
    let rErrs = typeCheck env r in
    listAppend here (listAppend lErrs rErrs)
  | EEq l r ->
    let lErrs = typeCheck env l in
    let rErrs = typeCheck env r in
    listAppend lErrs rErrs
  | ELt l r ->
    let lErrs = typeCheck env l in
    let rErrs = typeCheck env r in
    listAppend lErrs rErrs
  | EGt l r ->
    let lErrs = typeCheck env l in
    let rErrs = typeCheck env r in
    listAppend lErrs rErrs
  | EApp f x ->
    listAppend (typeCheck env f) (typeCheck env x)
  | ELam _ body -> typeCheck env body
  | ELetIn _ rhs body ->
    listAppend (typeCheck env rhs) (typeCheck env body)
  | EIf c t el ->
    let ce = typeCheck env c in
    let te = typeCheck env t in
    let ee = typeCheck env el in
    let branchErrs = checkIfBranches env t el in
    listAppend (listAppend (listAppend ce te) ee) branchErrs
  | EMatch scrut _ bodies ->
    let sErrs = typeCheck env scrut in
    let bodiesErrs = typeCheckBodies env bodies in
    listAppend sErrs bodiesErrs
  | ENil -> []
  | ECons h t -> listAppend (typeCheck env h) (typeCheck env t)

-- Walk every arm body in a match expression under the same outer env
-- (pattern binders are NOT re-introduced in this slice — the HM pass
-- is only interested in arithmetic / if mismatches, and those never
-- rely on pattern-bound names in the test corpus). Concatenates each
-- body's errors.
fn typeCheckBodies(env TypeEnv)(bodies List[Expr]) List[Str] =
  | b :: rest -> listAppend (typeCheck env b) (typeCheckBodies env rest)
  | _ -> []

-- Add one `Param` binding to a `TypeEnv`. Pulled out of a lambda into
-- its own top-level fn because 15's parser doesn't fold a `match`
-- inside a `\acc p. ...` body — keeping the match at fn-body scope
-- sidesteps that dispatcher ambiguity.
fn seedParamOne(env TypeEnv)(p Param) TypeEnv =
  | MkParam n tr -> typeEnvAdd env n (TyName (typeRefName tr))

-- Seed a `TypeEnv` with one binding per fn param. `MkParam name (TR
-- tyName)` becomes `name -> TyName tyName`. This is all the HM env
-- ever gets in this slice — no top-level let / fn bindings, no
-- cross-decl resolution, because the pass only cares whether fn
-- params (typed at declaration) are misused inside their own body.
fn seedParams(env TypeEnv)(ps List[Param]) TypeEnv =
  listFold (\acc p. seedParamOne acc p) env ps

-- Run the HM pass on a single `FnDecl` body. Seeds a fresh `TypeEnv`
-- from the fn's params, then walks the body with `typeCheck`.
fn hmFn(fd FnDecl) List[Str] =
  | MkFn _ params _ body ->
    let env0 = MkTypeEnv [] in
    let env = seedParams env0 params in
    typeCheck env body

-- Run the HM pass on a single decl. Only `DFn` contributes (fn bodies
-- are the only place 7.9a's tight HM pass has anything to say).
-- `DExport inner` unwraps one level so `export fn ...` still gets
-- checked. Every other decl shape contributes nothing.
fn hmDecl(d Decl) List[Str] =
  | DFn fd -> hmFn fd
  | DExport inner -> hmDecl inner
  | _ -> []

fn hmCheck(decls List[Decl]) List[Str] =
  | d :: rest -> listAppend (hmDecl d) (hmCheck rest)
  | _ -> []

-- Top-level elaborator entry. First collects top-level names into a
-- fresh env AND a type-to-ctors index, then runs the name-resolution
-- pass, the exhaustiveness pass, and the Phase 7.9a minimal HM pass
-- under those indices and concatenates all three error lists. Errors
-- are emitted in a stable order: E002 (name resolution) first, then
-- E003 (exhaustiveness), then E001 (HM type mismatches) — matching
-- the expected stdout of the runtime test.
-- Phase 7.9e: seed the elaborator env with the stdlib builtin names the
-- bootstrap compiler itself relies on, so `fn greet(n Str) Str =
-- strConcat "hi " n` no longer fires `E002 UnboundVar strConcat`. The
-- list mirrors the host's `Elaborator.fs` builtin table — if a name is
-- callable from the real ll-lang compiler, it should be callable from
-- the self-hosted compiler's input programs too. Still a flat name
-- list (no types) — the minimal HM pass in `hmCheck` skips unknowns.
fn stdlibNames(_ignored Int) List[Str] =
  ["strConcat" "strLen" "strChars" "strFromChars" "strToInt" "print" "printfn" "listMap" "listLen" "listAppend" "listIsEmpty" "listFold" "listReverse" "readFile" "true" "false" "charToInt" "charIsDigit" "charIsSpace" "intToStr"]

fn elaborate(decls List[Decl]) List[Str] =
  let env0 = MkEnv (stdlibNames 0) in
  let env = collectDecls env0 decls in
  let types = collectTypes decls in
  let nameErrs = checkDecls env decls in
  let exhErrs = exhaustivenessCheck types decls in
  let hmErrs = hmCheck decls in
  listAppend (listAppend nameErrs exhErrs) hmErrs

-- Render the error list as one message per line. Empty list prints a
-- single `no errors` sentinel so the runtime can tell a clean
-- elaborate apart from an elaborate that printed nothing at all.
fn showErrs(errs List[Str]) Str =
  if listIsEmpty errs then "no errors"
  else listFold (\acc s. if strLen acc == 0 then s else strConcat (strConcat acc "\n") s) "" errs

-- ---------- F# code emission (Phase 7.9b) -------------------------------
--
-- Phase 7.9b turns 20-bootstrap-compiler.lll from a 3-stage error
-- reporter (parse + elab + HM) into a true 4-stage compiler that
-- emits F# source as its final output. The codegen pass walks the
-- parser's `Decl` / `Expr` / `Pat` AST and produces an F# source
-- string with the same shape as the F# host's `Codegen.fs` module:
-- a `module <path>` header, an auto-generated stdlib prelude block,
-- the `type` decls (sum bodies only), the non-main fn decls
-- collapsed into a `let rec ... and ...` group, and a final
-- `[<EntryPoint>]`-wrapped zero-param `main` fn.
--
-- The helpers are intentionally a flat port of the `showTExpr` /
-- `showDecl` family from `19-codegen-real.lll`, adapted to walk the
-- parser's richer 9-variant `Expr` AST (vs 19's 9-variant `TExpr`)
-- and the parser's `Decl` / `MkFn` / `MkParam` shape (vs 19's
-- 2-variant `TDecl`). Helpers are prefixed `emit*` to keep the
-- pretty-printer family (`showExpr` / `showDecl` / `showModule`) free.
--
-- Scope of the pass (only what the hardcoded `main` source needs):
--   * `EInt` / `EStr` / `EVar` / `EAdd` / `EApp`             — Phase 7.9b
--   * `ESub` / `EMul` / `EDiv` / `ELam` / `ELetIn` / `EIf`   — Phase 7.9b
--   * `EMatch` (parallel pat/body lists, single-line)         — Phase 7.9b
--   * `ETagged` (just emits the inner expr; tag is dropped)   — Phase 7.9b
--   * `Pat`: `PInt` / `PVar` / `PWild` / `PNil` / `PCons`     — Phase 7.9b
--   * `DType` (sum bodies only) / `DFn` / `DLet`              — Phase 7.9b
-- NOT in this slice: records, `DTag`, `DImport`, `DExport`, `TBRecord`,
-- spread patterns, escape handling in `EStr`, `Float` / `Char` / `Unit`,
-- proper `let rec` recursion-detection (we always emit `let rec` for
-- 2+ runs — the same simplification 19-codegen-real.lll uses).

-- ---- Pattern emission --------------------------------------------------

-- Render a `Pat` as its F# source form. Mirrors `showPat` in
-- 19-codegen-real.lll but extends it to the parser's full 6-variant
-- `Pat` (PInt / PVar / PWild / PNil / PCons / PCon). `PNil` -> `[]`,
-- `PCons h t` -> `(h :: t)` (recursive, parens unconditional).
-- Phase 7.9l added `PCon name args` -> `(Name arg1 arg2)` — F#
-- discriminated-union pattern shape, parens unconditional, each arg
-- prefixed by a single space.
fn emitPat(p Pat) Str =
  | PInt n -> strConcat (intToStr n) "L"
  | PStr s -> strConcat (strConcat "\"" s) "\""
  | PVar x -> x
  | PWild -> "_"
  | PNil -> "[]"
  | PCons h t -> strConcat "(" (strConcat (emitPat h) (strConcat " :: " (strConcat (emitPat t) ")")))
  | PCon name args -> strConcat "(" (strConcat name (strConcat (emitPatArgs args) ")"))

fn emitPatArgs(ps List[Pat]) Str =
  match ps with
    | [] -> ""
    | p :: rest -> strConcat " " (strConcat (emitPat p) (emitPatArgs rest))

-- ---- Expression emission -----------------------------------------------

-- `EInt n` -> `<n>L` (F# `int64` literal). ll-lang's `Int` maps to
-- F# `int64` so every literal carries the `L` suffix.
fn emitInt(n Int) Str = strConcat (intToStr n) "L"

-- Phase 7.9m: re-encode a single Char as it would appear inside an
-- F# string literal body. Symmetric to `decodeEscape` on the lexer
-- side: `\n` → `\n`, `\t` → `\t`, `\\` → `\\`, `\"` → `\"`. Other
-- chars pass through verbatim wrapped via `strFromChars`. Mirrors
-- `emitChar` but for string-context escape set (`\"` instead of
-- `\'`), so the emitted F# source is valid after the lexer decoded
-- any escape sequences in the original ll-lang source.
fn encodeStrEscape(c Char) Str =
  if c == '\n' then "\\n"
  else if c == '\t' then "\\t"
  else if c == '\\' then "\\\\"
  else if c == '"' then "\\\""
  else strFromChars [c]

-- Phase 7.9m: walk a list of decoded chars and re-encode each one
-- via `encodeStrEscape`, concatenating the results. Uses `listFold`
-- for linearity (same pattern as `showParams`, `showCtors`, etc).
fn emitStrBody(cs List[Char]) Str =
  listFold (\acc c. strConcat acc (encodeStrEscape c)) "" cs

-- Phase 7.9m: `EStr s` -> `"<re-encoded s>"`. Before 7.9m this just
-- wrapped `s` raw in quotes, which was fine while the lexer passed
-- `\n` through literally. Now the lexer decodes escapes to raw
-- bytes, so the emitter must invert the mapping — otherwise a raw
-- newline gets injected into the F# source mid-literal. Round-trip:
-- `"a\nb"` -> body chars `[a, <LF>, b]` -> emitted `"a\nb"`.
fn emitStr(s Str) Str =
  strConcat "\"" (strConcat (emitStrBody (strChars s)) "\"")

-- Phase 7.9j: re-encode a `Char` as an F#-compatible char literal.
-- The lexer decoded `'\n'` / `'\\'` / `'\''` / `'\t'` into raw
-- bytes; here we invert the mapping so the emitted F# source is
-- valid. Any other char is wrapped verbatim in single quotes.
fn emitChar(c Char) Str =
  if c == '\n' then "'\\n'"
  else if c == '\t' then "'\\t'"
  else if c == '\\' then "'\\\\'"
  else if c == '\'' then "'\\''"
  else strConcat (strConcat "'" (strFromChars [c])) "'"

-- Top-level expression dispatcher. Each non-leaf arm delegates to a
-- dedicated helper to keep the outer `match e` one level deep — same
-- "shallow spine" trick `19-codegen-real.lll`'s `showTExpr` uses.
fn emitExpr(e Expr) Str =
  | EInt n -> emitInt n
  | EStr s -> emitStr s
  | EChar c -> emitChar c
  | EVar x -> x
  | ETagged inner _ -> emitExpr inner
  | EAdd l r -> emitBin l r " + "
  | ESub l r -> emitBin l r " - "
  | EMul l r -> emitBin l r " * "
  | EDiv l r -> emitBin l r " / "
  | EEq l r -> emitBin l r " = "
  | ELt l r -> emitBin l r " < "
  | EGt l r -> emitBin l r " > "
  | EApp f x -> emitApp f x
  | ELam x body -> emitLam x body
  | ELetIn n e1 e2 -> emitLetIn n e1 e2
  | EIf c t el -> emitIfExpr c t el
  | EMatch scr ps bs -> emitMatchExpr scr ps bs
  | ENil -> "[]"
  | ECons h t -> strConcat "(" (strConcat (emitExpr h) (strConcat " :: " (strConcat (emitExpr t) ")")))

-- Shared shape for `+ - * /` arms: `(<l><op><r>)` with unconditional
-- parens (mirrors the host's binaryOp arm in `Codegen.fs`). Pulls the
-- operator into a `Str` argument so a single helper covers all four
-- arithmetic shapes — same trick as `19-codegen-real.lll`'s `showAdd`
-- but generalised to a 3-arg helper.
fn emitBin(l Expr)(r Expr)(op Str) Str =
  strConcat "(" (strConcat (emitExpr l) (strConcat op (strConcat (emitExpr r) ")")))

-- `EApp f a` -> `(<f> <a>)`. Same parens-unconditional rule as
-- `19-codegen-real.lll`'s `showApp`.
fn emitApp(f Expr)(a Expr) Str =
  strConcat "(" (strConcat (emitExpr f) (strConcat " " (strConcat (emitExpr a) ")")))

-- `ELam x body` -> `(fun <x> -> <body>)`. Single-param lambda only;
-- multi-param surface lambdas are encoded as nested ELam in the parser.
fn emitLam(x Str)(body Expr) Str =
  strConcat "(fun " (strConcat x (strConcat " -> " (strConcat (emitExpr body) ")")))

-- `ELetIn n e1 e2` -> `(let <n> = <e1> in <e2>)`. Inline form is
-- offside-rule-safe in any expression position (mirrors the host's
-- `Codegen.fs` line 162 area emission).
fn emitLetIn(n Str)(e1 Expr)(e2 Expr) Str =
  strConcat "(let " (strConcat n (strConcat " = " (strConcat (emitExpr e1) (strConcat " in " (strConcat (emitExpr e2) ")")))))

-- `EIf c t e` -> `(if <c> then <t> else <e>)`. Unconditionally
-- parenthesised so the if embeds cleanly in any parent expression.
fn emitIfExpr(c Expr)(t Expr)(el Expr) Str =
  strConcat "(if " (strConcat (emitExpr c) (strConcat " then " (strConcat (emitExpr t) (strConcat " else " (strConcat (emitExpr el) ")")))))

-- `EMatch scr ps bs` -> `(match <scr> with <arms>)`. Arms emitted on
-- a **single line** joined by spaces — same single-line trick as
-- `19-codegen-real.lll`'s `showMatch` and the host's `TEMatchOf` arm.
-- The parallel `ps`/`bs` lists are walked in lockstep by `emitArms`.
fn emitMatchExpr(scr Expr)(ps List[Pat])(bs List[Expr]) Str =
  strConcat "(match " (strConcat (emitExpr scr) (strConcat " with " (strConcat (emitArms ps bs) ")")))

-- Walk parallel `ps`/`bs` lists in lockstep, joining each arm into
-- `"| <p> -> <b>"` chunks separated by single spaces. Split into a
-- `head + tail` cons-helper so the outer `match ps` stays one level
-- deep. Robust against tail mismatches: if either list runs out we
-- silently stop (the parser's `parseArms` already enforces the
-- length invariant).
fn emitArms(ps List[Pat])(bs List[Expr]) Str =
  match ps with
    | p :: prest -> emitArmsCons p prest bs
    | _ -> ""

fn emitArmsCons(p Pat)(prest List[Pat])(bs List[Expr]) Str =
  match bs with
    | b :: brest ->
      let head = strConcat "| " (strConcat (emitPat p) (strConcat " -> " (emitExpr b))) in
      let rest = emitArms prest brest in
      if strLen rest == 0 then head else strConcat head (strConcat " " rest)
    | _ -> ""

-- ---- Type-arg + ctor emission ------------------------------------------

-- Render a single-letter type parameter as F# `'<n>` (e.g. `A` -> `'A`).
fn emitTypeParam(p Str) Str = strConcat "'" p

-- Primitive-name mapping helper: `Int` -> `int64`, `Str` -> `string`,
-- `Bool` -> `bool`, anything else passes through verbatim. Same map
-- as `19-codegen-real.lll`'s `showTypeArgName`.
fn emitTypeArgName(n Str) Str =
  if n == "Int" then "int64"
  else if n == "Str" then "string"
  else if n == "Bool" then "bool"
  else n

-- Render a single `TypeArg` as its F# type string. Mirrors
-- `19-codegen-real.lll`'s `showTypeArg`, extended for the parser's
-- richer 3-variant `TypeArg` (TAVar / TACon / TAApp). `TAApp head args`
-- emits as `head<a1, a2, ...>`; the bracket-form parametric
-- application is the same shape F# uses for generic types.
fn emitTypeArg(a TypeArg) Str =
  | TAVar v -> emitTypeParam v
  | TACon n -> emitTypeArgName n
  | TAApp head args ->
    let body = emitTypeArgsInner args in
    strConcat (emitTypeArgName head) (strConcat "<" (strConcat body ">"))

-- Comma-joined inner type-arg list for `TAApp`. Pulled out into its
-- own fn so the `TAApp` arm in `emitTypeArg` stays one line.
fn emitTypeArgsInner(args List[TypeArg]) Str =
  match args with
    | a :: rest -> emitTypeArgsInnerTail (emitTypeArg a) rest
    | _ -> ""

fn emitTypeArgsInnerTail(acc Str)(rest List[TypeArg]) Str =
  match rest with
    | a :: more -> emitTypeArgsInnerTail (strConcat acc (strConcat ", " (emitTypeArg a))) more
    | _ -> acc

-- Join a ctor's `List[TypeArg]` into the `T1 * T2 * ...` arg-tuple
-- body (without the leading `of `). Empty list returns "" — callers
-- branch on it via `emitCtorBody`.
fn emitCtorArgs(args List[TypeArg]) Str =
  match args with
    | a :: rest -> emitCtorArgsTail (emitTypeArg a) rest
    | _ -> ""

fn emitCtorArgsTail(acc Str)(rest List[TypeArg]) Str =
  match rest with
    | a :: more -> emitCtorArgsTail (strConcat acc (strConcat " * " (emitTypeArg a))) more
    | _ -> acc

-- Render a single `Ctor` as one indented arm line — `"    | Name"`
-- for zero-arg ctors, `"    | Name of T1 * T2"` otherwise. Same
-- shape as `19-codegen-real.lll`'s `showCtor` / `showCtorBody`.
fn emitCtorOne(c Ctor) Str =
  | MkCtor name args -> emitCtorBody name args

fn emitCtorBody(name Str)(args List[TypeArg]) Str =
  match args with
    | _ :: _ ->
      let argStr = emitCtorArgs args in
      strConcat "    | " (strConcat name (strConcat " of " argStr))
    | _ -> strConcat "    | " name

-- Fold a `List[Ctor]` into a single newline-joined string. Each arm
-- occupies its own indented line (the `    | ` prefix is supplied
-- by `emitCtorOne` so this joiner only needs `\n`).
fn emitCtorList(cs List[Ctor]) Str =
  match cs with
    | c :: rest -> emitCtorListTail (emitCtorOne c) rest
    | _ -> ""

fn emitCtorListTail(acc Str)(rest List[Ctor]) Str =
  match rest with
    | c :: more -> emitCtorListTail (strConcat acc (strConcat "\n" (emitCtorOne c))) more
    | _ -> acc

-- Render the `<'A, 'B>` segment of a type header. Empty list returns
-- "" so monomorphic types like `Shape` emit as `type Shape = ...`.
fn emitTypeParamsHeader(ps List[Str]) Str =
  match ps with
    | p :: rest -> strConcat "<" (strConcat (emitTypeParamsBody p rest) ">")
    | _ -> ""

fn emitTypeParamsBody(first Str)(rest List[Str]) Str =
  match rest with
    | p :: more -> strConcat (emitTypeParam first) (strConcat ", " (emitTypeParamsBody p more))
    | _ -> emitTypeParam first

-- ---- Decl emission -----------------------------------------------------

-- `type Name<'A, 'B> =\n    | Ctor1\n    | Ctor2 of T1 * T2 ...`.
-- Sum bodies only — records and wrapped types are out of scope.
fn emitTypeDeclBody(name Str)(prms List[Str])(ctors List[Ctor]) Str =
  let header = strConcat "type " (strConcat name (emitTypeParamsHeader prms)) in
  let arms = emitCtorList ctors in
  strConcat header (strConcat " =\n" arms)

fn emitTypeDecl(td TypeDecl) Str =
  | MkTypeDecl name prms ctors -> emitTypeDeclBody name prms ctors

-- Extract every `MkParam name _` name in declaration order. The F#
-- side ignores the parser's `TypeRef` annotations and lets F# infer
-- — same trick as `19-codegen-real.lll`'s `showFnDeclPlain` which
-- only takes a `List[Str]` of param names.
fn emitParamName(p Param) Str =
  | MkParam n _ -> n

fn emitParamNames(ps List[Param]) List[Str] =
  listFold (\acc p. listAppend acc [(emitParamName p)]) [] ps

-- Space-separated param-name string. Empty list returns "".
fn emitFnParams(ps List[Str]) Str =
  match ps with
    | p :: rest -> emitFnParamsTail p rest
    | _ -> ""

fn emitFnParamsTail(first Str)(rest List[Str]) Str =
  match rest with
    | p :: more -> strConcat first (strConcat " " (emitFnParamsTail p more))
    | _ -> first

-- True iff `name` is `"main"` AND `params` is empty. Used by the
-- decl walker to single out the entry-point fn so it gets the
-- `[<EntryPoint>]` wrapper instead of the plain `let` form. Mirrors
-- `19-codegen-real.lll`'s `isMainFn`.
fn emitIsMainFn(name Str)(params List[Param]) Bool =
  match params with
    | _ :: _ -> false
    | _ -> if name == "main" then true else false

-- `[<EntryPoint>]`-wrapped form for a zero-param `main` fn. F#
-- requires the entry point to have signature `string[] -> int`, so
-- we add a dummy `argv` parameter and a trailing `0` exit code.
-- Mirrors `19-codegen-real.lll`'s `showMainDecl`.
fn emitMainDecl(body Expr) Str =
  let header = "[<EntryPoint>]\nlet main (argv: string[]) =\n    " in
  let trailer = "\n    0" in
  strConcat header (strConcat (emitExpr body) trailer)

-- Plain `let <name> <params> = <body>` (or `let <name> = <body>`
-- when `ps` is empty). Used for singleton non-main fn runs.
fn emitFnPlain(name Str)(ps List[Str])(body Expr) Str =
  match ps with
    | _ :: _ ->
      let paramStr = emitFnParams ps in
      strConcat "let " (strConcat name (strConcat " " (strConcat paramStr (strConcat " = " (emitExpr body)))))
    | _ ->
      strConcat "let " (strConcat name (strConcat " = " (emitExpr body)))

-- `let rec <name> <params> = <body>` — head of a 2+ run.
fn emitFnFirst(name Str)(ps List[Str])(body Expr) Str =
  match ps with
    | _ :: _ ->
      let paramStr = emitFnParams ps in
      strConcat "let rec " (strConcat name (strConcat " " (strConcat paramStr (strConcat " = " (emitExpr body)))))
    | _ ->
      strConcat "let rec " (strConcat name (strConcat " = " (emitExpr body)))

-- `and <name> <params> = <body>` — every non-first fn in a 2+ rec
-- block. Same shape as `emitFnFirst` with the prefix swapped.
fn emitFnCont(name Str)(ps List[Str])(body Expr) Str =
  match ps with
    | _ :: _ ->
      let paramStr = emitFnParams ps in
      strConcat "and " (strConcat name (strConcat " " (strConcat paramStr (strConcat " = " (emitExpr body)))))
    | _ ->
      strConcat "and " (strConcat name (strConcat " = " (emitExpr body)))

-- Plain `let <name> = <body>` for `DLet` module-level decls. The
-- parser's `MkLet name body` carries no params (let decls don't
-- bind functions in this slice).
fn emitLetDeclBody(name Str)(body Expr) Str =
  strConcat "let " (strConcat name (strConcat " = " (emitExpr body)))

fn emitLetDecl(ld LetDecl) Str =
  | MkLet name body -> emitLetDeclBody name body

-- Walk a single decl and emit its **standalone** F# source rendering
-- (no recursion grouping — that's `emitFnGroup`'s job). Routes a
-- `DFn` to either `emitMainDecl` (for the zero-param main entry
-- point) or `emitFnPlain` (for every other fn). `DType` / `DLet`
-- pass through to their dedicated emitters; `DTag` / `DImport` /
-- `DExport` are out of scope and emit a comment marker so the
-- output stays well-formed.
fn emitDeclStandalone(d Decl) Str =
  | DType td -> emitTypeDecl td
  | DLet ld -> emitLetDecl ld
  | DFn fd -> emitDeclStandaloneFn fd
  | DTag name -> strConcat "// tag " name
  | DImport path -> strConcat "// import " path
  | DExport inner -> emitDeclStandalone inner

fn emitDeclStandaloneFn(fd FnDecl) Str =
  | MkFn name params _ body ->
    let pnames = emitParamNames params in
    if emitIsMainFn name params then emitMainDecl body
    else emitFnPlain name pnames body

-- Dispatch a single `DFn` to `emitFnFirst`. Used by `emitFnGroup`'s
-- head emission. Returns "" for non-`DFn` inputs (defensive).
fn emitDeclFnFirst(d Decl) Str =
  | DFn fd -> emitDeclFnFirstUnwrap fd
  | _ -> ""

fn emitDeclFnFirstUnwrap(fd FnDecl) Str =
  | MkFn name params _ body ->
    let pnames = emitParamNames params in
    emitFnFirst name pnames body

-- Dispatch a single `DFn` to `emitFnCont`. Same split shape as
-- `emitDeclFnFirst`.
fn emitDeclFnCont(d Decl) Str =
  | DFn fd -> emitDeclFnContUnwrap fd
  | _ -> ""

fn emitDeclFnContUnwrap(fd FnDecl) Str =
  | MkFn name params _ body ->
    let pnames = emitParamNames params in
    emitFnCont name pnames body

-- Emit a 2+ run of consecutive non-main `DFn` decls as a single
-- `let rec ... and ...` block. Singleton runs fall through to
-- `emitDeclStandalone` via the dispatch in `emitFlushPending`.
-- Mirrors `19-codegen-real.lll`'s `showFnGroup` / `showFnGroupCons`.
fn emitFnGroup(fns List[Decl]) Str =
  match fns with
    | d :: rest -> emitFnGroupCons d rest
    | _ -> ""

fn emitFnGroupCons(first Decl)(rest List[Decl]) Str =
  let head = emitDeclFnFirst first in
  let tails = listMap emitDeclFnCont rest in
  let tailStr = emitJoinLines tails in
  if strLen tailStr == 0 then head else strConcat head (strConcat "\n" tailStr)

-- Newline-joined fold over a `List[Str]`. Same shape as
-- `19-codegen-real.lll`'s `joinLines` and `showErrs` above. Used
-- inside `emitFnGroup` to glue the `and ...` continuation lines.
fn emitJoinLines(xs List[Str]) Str =
  listFold (\acc s. if strLen acc == 0 then s else strConcat (strConcat acc "\n") s) "" xs

-- Blank-line-joined variant for stitching the module header,
-- prelude block, and per-decl blocks together with `\n\n` separators.
fn emitJoinBlocks(xs List[Str]) Str =
  listFold (\acc s. if strLen acc == 0 then s else strConcat (strConcat acc "\n\n") s) "" xs

-- Blank-line-append: glue `block` onto `acc` with `\n\n` if `acc`
-- is non-empty, else just return `block`. Used by the grouping
-- walker's incremental append pattern.
fn emitAppendBlock(block Str)(acc Str) Str =
  if strLen acc == 0 then block else strConcat acc (strConcat "\n\n" block)

-- ---- Module decl grouping walker ---------------------------------------
--
-- Walks a `List[Decl]` left-to-right and stitches them into a single
-- blank-line-joined string honouring the same grouping rules as
-- `19-codegen-real.lll`'s `splitAndShowDecls`:
--   * Every `DType` is its own block.
--   * Every `DLet` is its own block.
--   * Every zero-param `main` fn is its own block via `emitMainDecl`.
--   * Singleton non-main fn runs emit via `emitFnPlain`.
--   * 2+ consecutive non-main fns collapse into one
--     `let rec ... and ...` block via `emitFnGroup`.
--   * `DTag` / `DImport` are emitted as F# comments (out of scope).
--   * `DExport inner` recurses one level so an exported fn still
--     gets the right grouping.
--
-- Carries two accumulators: `pending` is the reversed list of
-- non-main DFns currently being collected into a potential rec
-- group; `acc` is the accumulated blank-line-joined output. Boundary
-- decls (anything that isn't a non-main DFn) flush `pending` first,
-- then append themselves to `acc`. End-of-list also flushes `pending`.

fn emitFlushPendingNonEmpty(fns List[Decl])(acc Str) Str =
  match fns with
    | d :: rest ->
      let block =
        match rest with
          | _ :: _ -> emitFnGroup fns
          | _ -> emitDeclStandalone d
      in
      emitAppendBlock block acc
    | _ -> acc

fn emitFlushPending(pending List[Decl])(acc Str) Str =
  match pending with
    | _ :: _ -> emitFlushPendingNonEmpty (listReverse pending) acc
    | _ -> acc

-- Main-boundary branch: flush pending, emit the main fn, keep walking.
fn emitStepMain(d Decl)(rest List[Decl])(pending List[Decl])(acc Str) Str =
  let acc2 = emitFlushPending pending acc in
  let mainBlock = emitDeclStandalone d in
  let acc3 = emitAppendBlock mainBlock acc2 in
  emitGroupLoop rest [] acc3

-- DFn branch dispatch: main → boundary; non-main → push onto pending.
fn emitStepFn(d Decl)(name Str)(params List[Param])(rest List[Decl])(pending List[Decl])(acc Str) Str =
  if emitIsMainFn name params then emitStepMain d rest pending acc
  else emitGroupLoop rest (d :: pending) acc

-- Per-step dispatch: look at the head decl and decide whether to
-- push it onto `pending` or flush + emit it as its own block.
fn emitStep(d Decl)(rest List[Decl])(pending List[Decl])(acc Str) Str =
  match d with
    | DFn fd -> emitStepFnUnwrap d fd rest pending acc
    | DType _ -> emitStepBoundary d rest pending acc
    | DLet _ -> emitStepBoundary d rest pending acc
    | DTag _ -> emitStepBoundary d rest pending acc
    | DImport _ -> emitStepBoundary d rest pending acc
    | DExport inner -> emitStep inner rest pending acc

-- Unwrap the `MkFn` to grab `name` / `params` for the main-vs-non-main
-- decision in `emitStepFn`.
fn emitStepFnUnwrap(d Decl)(fd FnDecl)(rest List[Decl])(pending List[Decl])(acc Str) Str =
  match fd with
    | MkFn name params _ _ -> emitStepFn d name params rest pending acc

-- Generic boundary step: flush pending, emit `d` as a standalone
-- block, keep walking. Shared by `DType` / `DLet` / `DTag` / `DImport`.
fn emitStepBoundary(d Decl)(rest List[Decl])(pending List[Decl])(acc Str) Str =
  let acc2 = emitFlushPending pending acc in
  let block = emitDeclStandalone d in
  let acc3 = emitAppendBlock block acc2 in
  emitGroupLoop rest [] acc3

fn emitGroupLoop(decls List[Decl])(pending List[Decl])(acc Str) Str =
  match decls with
    | d :: rest -> emitStep d rest pending acc
    | _ -> emitFlushPending pending acc

fn emitGroupedDecls(decls List[Decl]) Str =
  emitGroupLoop decls [] ""

-- ---- F# stdlib prelude block -------------------------------------------
--
-- Minimal 5-binding subset of the host's `fsharpPreludeCore`. Same
-- selection as `19-codegen-real.lll`'s `fsharpPrelude`. Wrapped in a
-- single-arg fn taking an ignored `Int` to dodge ll-lang's lack of
-- zero-param fn-call surface (callers pass `0`).
fn emitPrelude(_ignored Int) Str =
  let l1 = "// --- ll-lang stdlib prelude (auto-generated) ---" in
  let l2 = "let listMap f xs = List.map f xs" in
  let l3 = "let listLen (xs: 'a list) : int64 = int64 (List.length xs)" in
  let l4 = "let strLen (s: string) : int64 = int64 s.Length" in
  let l5 = "let strConcat (a: string) (b: string) = a + b" in
  let l6 = "let print (s: string) = System.Console.Write(s)" in
  let l7 = "// --- end prelude ---" in
  let lines = [l1 l2 l3 l4 l5 l6 l7] in
  emitJoinLines lines

-- ---- Top-level module emission -----------------------------------------

-- Render the full F# source file: `module <path>` header, blank
-- line, prelude block, blank line, then every decl rendered via
-- `emitGroupedDecls`. Same shape as `19-codegen-real.lll`'s
-- `showModule` / `showModuleBody`.
fn emitModuleBody(path Str)(decls List[Decl]) Str =
  let header = strConcat "module " path in
  let prelude = emitPrelude 0 in
  let declBlock = emitGroupedDecls decls in
  let parts = [header prelude declBlock] in
  emitJoinBlocks parts

fn emitModule(m Module) Str =
  | MkModule path decls -> emitModuleBody path decls

-- ---------- Driver -------------------------------------------------------
--
-- Phase 7.9c: the driver now **reads the source from a file** via
-- `readFile` instead of a hardcoded string literal — the first real
-- step toward `compiler₁ = compile(compiler.lll)`. The content of
-- `20a-bootstrap-input.lll` is a deliberately **clean** ll-lang
-- module (no unbound vars, no non-exhaustive matches, no type
-- mismatches) so the elaborator's combined error list is empty and
-- `main` proceeds to the codegen pass and prints the emitted F#
-- source. The 7.9a error-reporter path is preserved as a fallback
-- for non-empty error lists.
--
-- Source (read from `spec/examples/valid/20a-bootstrap-input.lll`):
--   module Examples.Clean
--   type Maybe A = Some A | None
--   fn inc(x Int) Int = x + 1
--   fn greet() Str = "hello"
--   fn main() Int = inc 1
--
-- Walk-through:
--   * `type Maybe A = Some A | None` declares a parametric sum type
--     with one arg-bearing ctor and one nullary ctor. The codegen
--     pass emits a `type Maybe<'A> =\n    | Some of 'A\n    | None`
--     block.
--   * `fn inc(x Int) Int = x + 1` and `fn greet() Str = "hello"` are
--     the two non-main fns; the grouping walker collapses them into
--     a single `let rec inc x = (x + 1L)\nand greet = "hello"` block.
--   * `fn main() Int = inc 1` is the zero-param entry point; the
--     codegen pass wraps it in `[<EntryPoint>]\nlet main (argv:
--     string[]) =\n    (inc 1L)\n    0`.
--
-- Pipeline shape:
--   readFile -> tokenize -> parseModule -> elaborate -> if errors
--   then printfn errs else printfn (emitModule mod). The error-
--   reporter path stays in for the day a regression reintroduces a
--   stale source.
fn main() =
  -- Path is relative to the repo root; `lllc run` (and the test
  -- harness) must set its working directory to the repo root.
  let src = readFile "spec/examples/valid/20a-bootstrap-input.lll" in
  let toks = tokenize src in
  let mod = parseModule toks in
  match mod with
    | MkModule _ decls ->
      let errs = elaborate decls in
      if listIsEmpty errs then printfn (emitModule mod)
      else printfn (showErrs errs)
